window.SFW_SEARCH = [{"t":"Documentation","u":"docs/index.html","s":"Get started","h":[["Start here","start-here"],["How StockFW is put together","how-stockfw-is-put-together"],["How to read these pages","how-to-read-these-pages"],["Versions","versions"],["The longer document","the-longer-document"]],"x":"StockFW documentation StockFW is a desktop firewall manager for Windows and Linux. It does not filter packets itself. It configures the firewall engine your operating system already has, shows you every connection that engine allowed or blocked, keeps your policy as plain text, and lets you try a change before you make it. Start here# Install on Windows: the installer, the elevated shortcut, and enabling monitoring. Install on Linux: the tarball, sudo and its search path, and the command line. Your first ten minutes: check protection, watch events, add a rule, apply it, ask the simulator a question. How StockFW is put together# Four parts, and knowing them makes every page of the app predictable. The engine Your operating system’s firewall: the Windows Filtering Platform behind Windows Defender Firewall, or netfilter behind iptables on Linux. StockFW adds and removes rules in it through supported tools and never replaces it. If StockFW is closed or crashes, the engine keeps enforcing whatever was last applied. The policy What you want the engine to do, stored as one small comma-separated file per rule kind in your profile. The Policy page, the Copilot and the command line all edit the same files. Saving a rule does not change the firewall; Apply does. The events What the engine decided, read from the log it already writes: Security events 5156 and 5157 on Windows, the kernel ring buffer on Linux. StockFW never sees packets, only verdicts, and it shows them as they are. The gate Every path that could change the firewall, the Apply button, an event’s right-click menu, a Copilot suggestion, Sentinel’s auto-block, stockfw apply, goes through one function. It measures the change against what is live, records what it applied, rolls back if the engine refuses, and writes one audit line. There is no second door. How to read these pages# Claims about behaviour carry a stamp. A stamp is a promise about evidence, not decoration: Verified test name An automated test in the repository proves it. The name is the test, so you can run it: most live in port/tests/ and in tests/test-core.sh. Measured a number A number from an experiment that was run, such as the 150-second memory soak. Built for 1.0.7 Done in the 1.0.7 development code on main and covered by its tests; not in the 1.0.5 release you can download today. In progress for 1.0.7 Being written for 1.0.7. The shape is decided; details may still move. Planned Intended. Not built, not promised for a date. Known gap A limit StockFW has today, written down so it is not mistaken for a feature. Where the two platforms differ, a badge says which one a sentence is about: Windows Linux. Everything without a badge applies to both. Versions# VersionWhereStatus 1.0.5DownloadThe current release, 11 October 2026. 1.0.6merged into mainNot packaged. Activity graphs, the command line, the dashboard. The 1.0.7 work now sits on top of it. 1.0.7branch mainIn development. These pages describe it, with the stamps above marking what is finished. The longer document# These pages explain how to use StockFW. The reasoning behind it, the threat model with file-and-line evidence, every defect found and how it was fixed, and the measurements, are in the architecture and security document in the repository, one chapter per release. When these pages and that document disagree, the document is right and this site has a bug; please report it."},{"t":"Activity","u":"docs/activity.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["The controls","the-controls"],["Policy activity","policy-activity"],["Network I/O","network-i-o"],["Why the grey band","why-the-grey-band"],["Reading a point","reading-a-point"],["How the data is kept","how-the-data-is-kept"]],"x":"Activity Two graphs over the last 15 minutes to 24 hours: how many connections your policy allowed and blocked per minute, and how many bytes per second crossed your adapters. The page is new in 1.0.6. Fifteen minutes of a staged port scan arriving while a download runs. The grey band is time before the app opened. The controls# Range: 15 min, 1 hour, 6 hours, 24 hours. Both graphs follow it. Adapter: all adapters together, or one. It affects the Network I/O graph and the throughput tile. Show as table: every interval as a row, for reading exact numbers or copying them. The three tiles summarise the range: blocked and allowed counts for the last hour and the last 24 hours, with “counting since 16:23” when the app has been open for less than that, and the current throughput in and out. Policy activity# Events per minute, allowed in blue and blocked in red. It counts exactly what the Events page lists, after that page’s filters and its duplicate check, and it counts each event at the event’s own time, not when the app happened to read it. That matters on Windows, where the first poll delivers a batch of recent history: those events land in their own minutes, and anything older than the app is dropped into the no-data band rather than piled into the current minute as a false spike. Verified fs-activity-smoke On Linux in 1.0.5 and 1.0.6 the allowed line is not drawn and the card says so, because the kernel log carried only drops and rejects, and a flat allowed line would have claimed that nothing was allowed. Since 1.0.7 the policy logs the first packet of each new allowed connection, so the line is real. Built for 1.0.7 Network I/O# Bytes per second received and sent, from the adapters’ own cumulative counters: GetIfTable2 on Windows, /proc/net/dev on Linux. No packet capture, no trace session, no elevation. A counter that goes backwards (a driver reload) starts a new baseline rather than drawing negative traffic. Verified test-core.sh Why the grey band# A graph that starts at zero looks like a quiet night. StockFW did not observe anything before it was opened, so that stretch is shaded “no data” and never drawn as a line. The newest point is partial, and its rate divides by the seconds that have actually elapsed, so the last sample is not drawn artificially low. Verified fs-activity-smoke Reading a point# Move the mouse over a graph for a crosshair and a tooltip with the values at that interval; or focus the graph and use the arrow keys. Each graph has one y axis with round gridlines, time ticks spaced for their labels, a legend and a label at the end of each line, and the colours were checked for contrast in light mode: allowed and received #2a78d6, blocked #e34948, sent #eb6834. How the data is kept# Counters are kept in 10-second slots, 8,640 per series (24 hours), at most 24 series, about 1.6 MB when full, so a week in the tray costs the same memory as an hour. Nothing is written to disk; the graphs are about this session. Verified test-core.sh The dashboard draws the same two graphs from the same code, and can backfill from the kernel log so its history starts before it was opened."},{"t":"Background and the tray","u":"docs/background.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["Turning it on","turning-it-on"],["The icon and its tooltip","the-icon-and-its-tooltip"],["The menu","the-menu"],["What runs in the background","what-runs-in-the-background"],["Where the icon cannot appear","where-the-icon-cannot-appear"],["Starting at login","starting-at-login"]],"x":"Background and the tray The firewall engine runs whether StockFW is open or not; StockFW in the background is what keeps watching events, learning behaviour and, in auto mode, letting Sentinel act. The tray icon is how you know it is there and how you get it back. Turning it on# Two settings on the Interface preference page, both on by default: Show a tray icon and Minimize to the tray. With both on, closing or minimising the window hides it and StockFW keeps running. With the icon off, closing the window quits the program; StockFW will not hide a window it cannot give back, because a background process with no way to reopen it would be a zombie with root rights. Verified ui-tray-smoke The icon and its tooltip# The tooltip reads StockFW, Protected or StockFW, Not protected, with the engine’s name, from the same state the Overview shows; when the state cannot be read it says Firewall state is unknown rather than guessing. The icon follows the state too, so the answer to “is my firewall running?” is one glance. The menu# Open StockFW brings the window back. About StockFW shows the version and credits. Quit StockFW ends the program. It does not stop the firewall: the engine keeps enforcing whatever was last applied, exactly as it would if StockFW crashed. To stop filtering, open the window and press Stop firewall, or run sudo stockfw stop. Coming in 1.0.7 A richer tray menu: the state, the last few blocks, Start and Stop, and the Sentinel mode, without opening the window. Quit stays a plain quit. What runs in the background# Event monitoring, and everything fed by it: the Activity counters, the Copilot’s baseline, Sentinel’s detectors. Sentinel’s ten-minute expiry timer for auto-blocks. In 1.0.7, the hourly check for threat-intel feed updates. In progress for 1.0.7 Memory stays flat: the Activity series are fixed-size rings, the Events list keeps 2,000 rows, the Copilot keeps 200 insights, and a 150-second soak at full event rate found no growth. Measured 150-second soak, docs chapter 9 Where the icon cannot appear# A tray needs a tray host. GNOME has not shipped one since 3.26; the AppIndicator extension restores it, and KDE, XFCE, MATE, Cinnamon and Windows have one built in. Without a host the icon simply does not show, the window keeps working, and closing it quits, so nothing is lost. The project’s own tray test fails on a headless machine for this reason, and is expected to. Known gap Starting at login# StockFW does not install itself as a login item or a service. On Windows the elevated shortcut needs a UAC prompt, which a silent start cannot give, so an autostart entry would start the unprivileged copy. On Linux a desktop autostart entry under ~/.config/autostart/ runs as you, not as root, with the same limits. If you want the firewall itself in force at boot, see what happens at reboot."},{"t":"Command line","u":"docs/cli.html","s":"Command line","h":[["Before you start: whose policy?","before-you-start-whose-policy"],["status","status"],["policy list, add, remove","policy"],["apply, start, stop","apply"],["simulate","simulate"],["events","events"],["connections, interfaces","connections"],["dashboard","dashboard"],["Output conventions","output-conventions"],["Environment variables","environment-variables"],["Coming in 1.0.7","coming-in-1-0-7"],["Tests","tests"]],"x":"Command line stockfw is the desktop app without the display: the same policy files, the same simulator, the same apply gate. A rule added here is the rule the Policy page shows, and an apply here writes the same audit line. Linux only in 1.0.6; a Windows build is intended. Planned stockfw --helpCopyUsage: stockfw [--json] [--no-color] [-q] <command> [arguments] StockFW from the command line: the same policy, simulator and apply gate as the desktop app, without a display. Commands: status Firewall state, policy and monitoring at a glance policy list|add|remove Read and edit the traffic policy apply [--dry-run] Push the saved policy to the firewall (root) start | stop Start or stop the firewall (root) simulate \"<traffic>\" What-if: would this traffic be allowed or blocked? events [-n N] [-f] Recent firewall events; -f follows live (root) connections Active TCP connections and their programs interfaces [--watch] Network adapters with traffic counters and rates dashboard [--port N] Serve the local web dashboard and open it help [command] Help for one command version Print the version Global options: --json Machine-readable output (one JSON document; NDJSON for -f) --no-color Plain output (also honoured: NO_COLOR, TERM=dumb, pipes) -q, --quiet Only errors and the essential result -h, --help This help; -V, --version the version Exit status: 0 success 2 usage error 5 simulate: BLOCKED 1 failure 3 needs root privileges Policy files live in ~/.stockfw (or /etc/stockfw when run as root) -- the same files the desktop app edits. Nothing here talks to the network: the dashboard listens on 127.0.0.1 only. Before you start: whose policy?# As your own user the policy lives in ~/.stockfw; as root, in /etc/stockfw. A rule added without sudo is not the rule sudo stockfw apply pushes. Pick one identity and keep to it; the simplest is sudo for everything, so the app, the command line and the firewall all read /etc/stockfw. For a script that must not depend on who runs it, set STOCKFW_RULES_DIR. sudo on Debian and Kali does not search /usr/local/bin, so write sudo /usr/local/bin/stockfw or add an alias. See Install on Linux. status# TerminalCopy$ sudo stockfw status StockFW 1.0.6 Linux (iptables) Firewall ● running IPv6 filtered Policy 9 rules in /etc/stockfw 3 inbound-allow-from 2 inbound-allow-service 1 inbound-forward 1 outbound-allow-to 1 outbound-deny-to 1 outbound-deny-service Apply mode live Monitoring available (stockfw events -f, stockfw dashboard) Adapters eth0 192.168.1.20 ↓ 84.5 MB ↑ 270.4 MB Connections 16 active Reading the firewall state needs root; without it the state is reported as unknown rather than guessed. Apply mode reads audit-only or locked when a hold is on. With --json: JSONCopy{\"version\":\"1.0.6\",\"backend\":\"linux\",\"state\":\"running\", \"ipv6\":{\"state\":\"filtered\",\"detail\":\"IPv4 and IPv6 are filtered by the same policy.\"}, \"root\":true,\"rules_dir\":\"/etc/stockfw\",\"policy_saved\":true,\"malformed_rules\":0, \"rules\":{\"inbound-allow-from\":3,\"inbound-allow-service\":2,\"inbound-forward\":1, \"outbound-allow-to\":1,\"outbound-allow-from\":0,\"outbound-allow-service\":0, \"outbound-deny-to\":1,\"outbound-deny-from\":0,\"outbound-deny-service\":1}, \"apply_hold\":null,\"monitoring_available\":true,\"connections\":16, \"interfaces\":[{\"device\":\"eth0\",\"type\":\"Ethernet\",\"ip\":\"192.168.1.20\",\"up\":true, \"received\":84498082,\"sent\":270438518}]} policy list, add, remove# TerminalCopy$ sudo stockfw policy add inbound-allow-service SSH 22 192.168.1.0/24 --comment \"admin LAN\" + inbound-allow-service SSH 22 192.168.1.0/24 Saved in /etc/stockfw. Not live yet: run `sudo stockfw apply'. $ sudo stockfw policy add outbound-allow-to 192.0.2.10 --both --comment \"backup server\" + outbound-allow-to 192.0.2.10 + inbound-allow-from 192.0.2.10 Saved in /etc/stockfw. Not live yet: run `sudo stockfw apply'. $ sudo stockfw policy list inbound-allow-from (3) 1 192.168.1.1 [IPv4 only] # router 2 2001:db8:1::/48 [IPv6 only] # office v6 3 192.0.2.10 [IPv4 only] # backup server inbound-allow-service (2) 1 SSH port 22 for 192.168.1.0/24 [IPv4 only] # admin LAN 2 HTTPS port 443 for Anyone [IPv4 + IPv6] # web server inbound-forward (1) 1 Web port 8080 -> 192.168.1.30:80 [IPv4 only] # lab box outbound-allow-to (1) 1 192.0.2.10 [IPv4 only] # backup server outbound-deny-to (1) 1 198.51.100.66 [IPv4 only] # known bad host outbound-deny-service (1) 1 Telnet port 23 for Anyone [IPv4 + IPv6] # no cleartext logins $ sudo stockfw policy remove outbound-deny-to 1 - outbound-deny-to 198.51.100.66 Saved. Not live yet: run `sudo stockfw apply'. The kinds and their fields are those of the Policy page: inbound-allow-from HOST, inbound-allow-service SERVICE PORT FOR, inbound-forward SERVICE FIREWALL-PORT TO PORT, outbound-allow-to HOST, outbound-allow-from HOST, outbound-allow-service SERVICE PORT FOR, outbound-deny-to HOST, outbound-deny-from HOST, outbound-deny-service SERVICE PORT FOR. FOR is a host, a network or Anyone. --both adds the opposite-direction twin of an allow rule as one change. N in remove is the number printed by list. --kind KIND narrows the list. Validation fails closed, with the same rules as the app: TerminalCopy$ stockfw policy add inbound-allow-service SSH 80abc Anyone stockfw: error: not a valid inbound-allow-service rule: check the host, port or range stockfw: hint: hosts are addresses, networks (10.0.0.0/8) or \"Anyone\"; ports are 1-65535 or a range 1024:2048 $ echo $? 2 policy list --json gives every rule with its kind, number, fields, comment, covers ([\"ipv4\"], [\"ipv6\"] or both) and covers_why, the sentence the Policy page shows as a tooltip: JSONCopy{\"kind\":\"inbound-allow-service\",\"number\":1,\"service\":\"SSH\",\"port\":\"22\", \"target\":\"192.168.1.0/24\",\"to\":null,\"to_port\":null,\"comment\":\"admin LAN\", \"covers\":[\"ipv4\"], \"covers_why\":\"192.168.1.0/24 is an IPv4 range, so the rule covers IPv4 only. Add a second rule for the IPv6 range if there is one.\"} apply, start, stop# TerminalCopy$ sudo stockfw apply --dry-run Would apply: +1 added (1 allow, 0 block), -0 removed (0 allow, 0 block), =8 unchanged Nothing was changed (dry run). $ sudo stockfw apply Applied (only the changes): +1 added (1 allow, 0 block), -0 removed (0 allow, 0 block), =8 unchanged $ sudo stockfw apply Nothing to change: the policy already matches the firewall. apply goes through the gate described on the Policy page: only what changed is touched, a refused apply is rolled back to the last applied policy and the rollback is verified, and every attempt is one audit line with the source cli. When Audit-only or Lock is on in the app, apply records the change and leaves the firewall alone: “Held: audit-only mode is on. The firewall was left untouched.” Notes from the engine (“IPv6 is not filtered: ip6tables is not installed”) are printed after the result. start builds the firewall from the saved policy and starts it; stop removes StockFW’s own chains and nothing else. All three need root and exit 3 without it. simulate# TerminalCopy$ stockfw simulate \"allow ssh from 192.168.1.50 on port 22\" ALLOWED Inbound TCP connection from 192.168.1.50 to local port 22 (ssh) Packet inbound TCP port 22 from 192.168.1.50 Rule StockFW: Allow SSH (inbound, 192.168.1.0/24) Why Matched rule 'StockFW: Allow SSH (inbound, 192.168.1.0/24)' on port 22, which allows inbound traffic. A simulation only: nothing was written. $ echo $? 0 $ stockfw simulate \"outbound tcp to 198.51.100.66 port 443\" --json {\"input\":\"outbound tcp to 198.51.100.66 port 443\",\"verdict\":\"BLOCKED\", \"scenario\":\"Outbound TCP connection from local to 198.51.100.66 port 443 (unnamed application)\", \"direction\":\"outbound\",\"protocol\":\"TCP\",\"port\":443,\"remote\":\"198.51.100.66\", \"rule\":\"StockFW: Block outbound (198.51.100.66)\", \"reason\":\"Matched rule 'StockFW: Block outbound (198.51.100.66)', which blocks outbound traffic.\", \"suggestion\":\"Allow outbound TCP port 443 to 198.51.100.66.\"} $ echo $? 5 Exit 0 means ALLOWED, 5 means BLOCKED, so a script can branch without parsing. The sentence grammar is on the simulator pa"},{"t":"Copilot","u":"docs/copilot.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["What is on the page","what-is-on-the-page"],["The baseline","the-baseline"],["The insights","the-insights"],["Sentinel","sentinel"],["Auto-block, and what it may never do","auto-block-and-what-it-may-never-do"],["The audit trail","the-audit-trail"],["Honest limits","honest-limits"]],"x":"Copilot A local analyst that reads the same events the Events page shows, learns what each program normally does, and tells you when something steps outside that. It is a parser and a set of counters, not a language model: it has no network, no account and no prompt, so it works on an air-gapped machine and cannot be talked into anything. Two findings after a scan: a program using a port it never used, and a first contact with a new destination. Neither stages a rule by itself. What is on the page# Sentinel: Detect only or Detect + auto-block. Detect only is the default and reports without touching the policy. Run analysis looks at the current policy and the events seen so far and lists what it finds. Sentinel’s detectors run on every event regardless. Four tiles: Apps learned, Active insights, Events analyzed, Policy rules. Insights and suggestions: a table of findings with title, type, severity, description, program and status. Double-click a row for the full explanation. Apply selected rule stages the rule a finding suggests, after showing it; Clear insights empties the list; Undo auto-block lifts what Sentinel staged. What-if simulator: a question about traffic, answered against the saved policy. It has its own page. The baseline# Every event is attributed to a program (on Windows the event carries its path; on Linux the connection table does), and each program gets a profile: the ports it has used, the destinations it has contacted, the hours of the day it is active, and how many connections it makes per ten-minute window. The profile is written to behavior.csv in your profile folder and read back at the next start, so what was learned survives a restart. The profile is deliberately bounded: 256 programs, 256 ports and 1,024 destinations each. When a program reaches a cap its profile is marked partial, and the novelty findings (“first time on this port”) go quiet for it, because a truncated baseline cannot honestly claim “never seen before”. Findings about hours need four distinct hours of history first; findings about rate need eight completed windows and at least ten events in the window being judged. Verified test-core.sh The insights# TypeWhat it meansCarries a rule? New AppA program made its first connection.No Unusual PortA known program used a port it never used before.Suggests an allow for review Unusual DestA known program contacted a destination it never contacted before.Suggests an allow for review Unusual VolumeA program’s connection rate jumped far above its established rate.No Unusual TimeActivity at an hour the program has never been active at.No Rule Conflict, Redundant Rule, Missing Rule, OptimizationFindings about the policy itself: rules that contradict, duplicate or could be merged, and traffic no rule describes.Sometimes, always shown first SuspiciousBehaviour that matches the shape of malware traffic.No BeaconSentinel: metronomic outbound contact with one host. See below.A deny, the only kind that can auto-stage Fan-OutSentinel: a burst of distinct destinations, the shape of a scan or a spreading worm.Never: a line, not a judgement Severities are Info, Warning and Critical. The list keeps the newest 200 findings. A finding that suggests an allow is only ever a suggestion: nothing on this page can open the firewall without you pressing a button and reading the rule in a Yes/No dialog first. Verified test-adversarial.sh Sentinel# Sentinel watches outbound traffic for the two signatures of a program that is no longer working for you. It is egress-only by design: an inbound scan is the Events page’s business, and the engine already drops it. Beacon Six or more contacts with the same host and port, at a mean interval between 10 seconds and 10 minutes, with a coefficient of variation of 0.30 or less. A human clicking, a browser’s jittered keep-alives and git’s background fetches do not pass that regularity test; a command-and-control callback does. The finding is Critical and carries a deny rule for the destination. Fan-out Sixty distinct destinations within sixty seconds, or ten distinct destinations the firewall blocked within sixty seconds. The finding is Critical and deliberately carries no rule, because “block everything this program touched” is a decision for you, not for a counter. A package manager pulling from 59 mirrors reports nothing; the 60th reports a line. Both thresholds are pinned by the adversarial suite, which plays a blocked port scan, a single-host port sweep, a command-and-control beacon, a replay of a real incident, benign developer tools, an exfiltration burst and a failed apply through the detectors, and asserts that only the beacon would ever reach the firewall unattended. Verified test-adversarial.sh Auto-block, and what it may never do# With Sentinel set to Detect + auto-block, a beacon’s deny rule is staged into the policy and pushed through the apply gate without waiting for you. Because that is the one place where StockFW acts on its own, it is fenced on every side: Only a deny, only for a public stranger. Auto-block can tighten the policy, never loosen it. A destination that is loopback, link-local, multicast, a private LAN range, carrier NAT, a reserved range, or an IPv4-mapped IPv6 address standing for any of those, is never auto-blocked, and neither is anything that does not parse as an address. A destination this program has contacted three or more times before the current run is established, and established traffic is reported, never blocked. That is the browser-talking-to-a-CDN case. Verified test_sentinel_auto_gates Ten minutes. Every staged block expires ten minutes after it was staged. The staging record is written to the autoblocks file as it changes, so a block survives a restart with its original clock: a restart grants no fresh ten minutes, and a block that expired while StockFW was closed is taken out at the next start, with an audit line saying so. Verified fs-copilot-smoke No loops. Once a block is taken down, by expiry, by Undo, by your own Allow or by switching to Detect only, the same rule cannot be staged again in this session. The incident that shaped these rules was a ten-minute block that re-staged itself every ten minutes for two days. Your Allow wins. Allowing a destination from the Events page first lifts any Sentinel block for it, then pushes the allow, so both reach the firewall together. On Windows a block rule outranks an allow rule, so without this your Allow would have changed nothing on the wire. Detect means detect. Switching Sentinel out of auto mode takes every staged block down at once. Your rules are never touched. Sentinel’s rows carry a Sentinel: comment and are the only rows it will ever remove. In the Status column a staged rule reads Auto-blocked; the Undo auto-block button lifts the selected one at once. The audit trail# Every stage, push, undo, expiry, restore at start-up and mode switch is one line in audit.log: sentinel auto-block, sentinel auto-block is live, sentinel auto-block staged, not live, sentinel rule removed, sentinel auto-blocks restored, and the failure variants. The file is only ever appended to. Files and formats describes the line. Honest limits# On Linux the kernel log carries no program name, so profiles are keyed by what the connection table can attribute; a connection that closes before the next poll is counted without a program. Known gap Sentinel has no inbound scan detector, by design. The behaviour analysis is heuristic. It is explained in plain words so you can judge it; it is not a verdict about the program’s intent. An older About dialog still describes the Copilot as “AI-powered”. It is rule-based; the wording is being corrected. In progress for 1.0.7"},{"t":"Country and network-owner blocking","u":"docs/country-blocking.html","s":"Protection","h":[["Where the data comes from","where-the-data-comes-from"],["What “country” means","what-country-means"],["How it will work","how-it-will-work"],["What it is not","what-it-is-not"]],"x":"Country and network-owner blocking Some servers have no business talking to any country but their own, and some attacks come from a handful of networks. 1.0.7 adds two more kinds of threat-intel set, built from an offline database rather than a feed: every address registered to a country, and every address announced by a network owner (an autonomous system). Where this stands This is round two of the 1.0.7 threat-intel work, after blocklists. The data source is decided and its reader is being written In progress for 1.0.7; the picker and the sets follow. The database’s licence terms are being confirmed with its publisher before release, and a fallback source is chosen in case they cannot be. Where the data comes from# The IPFire Location database: one file, about 6 MB compressed, published daily, no account needed, with IPv4 and IPv6 in one structure. For every range it gives the country of registration, the announcing network (AS number and name), and flags for anonymous proxies, satellite providers and anycast networks. It is the same database the IPFire firewall uses for its own country blocking. The file is signed inside its header with ECDSA over a SHA-256 of its contents, and StockFW verifies that signature against a pinned public key before reading a single record. A version check uses a DNS record the publisher maintains, and a downloaded file older than the one already on disk is refused, so a replayed old database cannot roll the data back. The reader unpacks with a strict size cap and bounds-checks every offset in the header before it trusts it. If the licence cannot be confirmed in time, the fallback is DB-IP’s free country database with the iptoasn network-owner table, both unsigned, in which case StockFW relies on HTTPS, strict structure checks and keeping the last good copy. What “country” means# The country is where the address block is registered, from the regional registries, geofeeds and the publisher’s corrections. It is not a measurement of where a packet came from, and for a content network that announces one address from forty cities it cannot be. StockFW therefore says “registered in”, never “located in”, and the picker warns when a chosen country’s ranges include anycast networks: blocking such a country can block a service everyone uses, from everywhere. The same honesty applies to network owners: the AS name is the owner on record. How it will work# On the Threat Intel page, a picker with autocompletion over countries and network owners, each with its flag and the number of ranges it would add. A chosen entry becomes a set with a direction, like a feed. The sets are built, subtracted, loaded and reported exactly like blocklists: the same protected ranges, the same exceptions, the same transaction into inet stockfw_intel, the same state table, the same events and audit lines. A country block that would include your own network, your DNS servers or your SSH peer loses those addresses and says so in the notes. The lookup box, the What-if simulator and the Events page name the country or owner that blocked an address. stockfw intel gains the country and owner commands, with --json. The database updates on the same hourly check as the feeds, from the publisher only, after the same consent dialog. What it is not# Not a geofence for a laptop that travels, and not a substitute for authentication: an attacker who wants to reach you from a blocked country rents a server in an allowed one. It removes background noise and narrows the field, which is worth having, and the app will describe it that way."},{"t":"Dashboard","u":"docs/dashboard.html","s":"Command line","h":[["Start it","start-it"],["What is on the page","what-is-on-the-page"],["Backfill","backfill"],["Why it is safe to run","why-it-is-safe-to-run"],["The API","the-api"],["Coming in 1.0.7","coming-in-1-0-7"]],"x":"Dashboard A single web page, served by the stockfw binary itself, that shows the firewall’s state, the two Activity graphs, live events, connections, the policy and the what-if simulator in a browser. It is a window onto the firewall, not a way to change it. Linux Light theme. The page is one HTML file compiled into the binary; nothing is loaded from anywhere else. Start it# TerminalCopy$ sudo stockfw dashboard StockFW dashboard http://127.0.0.1:43977/#token=287cebbf9d83ed6af523f96b7cc3dd13 Listening 127.0.0.1:43977 only, read-only, this run's token Events Live, with history from the kernel log Ctrl-C stops the dashboard. Without --port the system picks a free port. The URL carries the token for this run in its fragment; the page moves it into session storage and strips it from the address bar, then sends it with every API call. Under sudo the browser is not started as root, so you open the printed URL yourself. --no-open never tries. Ctrl-C stops it; nothing is left behind. What is on the page# Tiles: firewall state (and whether IPv6 is in force), blocked and allowed counts for the chosen range, throughput now. Range picker, 15 minutes to 24 hours, and Policy activity and Network I/O graphs drawn by the same code as the Activity page, with the same no-data band, crosshair and keyboard navigation. Recent events, newest first, with a filter box, and Active connections with their programs. The dashboard hides its own sockets: they exist only because someone is looking. What-if simulator: the same grammar and verdicts as the app and the command line. Nothing is written. Policy: the saved rules, grouped by kind, with their Covers. A theme button: system, light or dark. Dark theme. The graph colours were checked for contrast in both. Backfill# The app’s Activity graphs start when the app starts. The dashboard can read the kernel log for the events StockFW’s rules logged before it was started, so its history begins before the browser was opened, and the no-data band is shorter or absent. The log stays the archive; the dashboard only reads it. Why it is safe to run# A page that shows your firewall is a page an attacker would like to read, or to use as a lever. The dashboard is designed so that neither works, and a test exercises each point. Verified test-cli.sh Loopback only It binds 127.0.0.1. There is no option to listen anywhere else; --bind does not exist, and asking for it prints why. Someone on your network cannot reach it. To use it from another machine, forward the port over SSH. A token for this run 128 bits from the kernel’s random source, new every start. Every /api/ call must carry it in an X-StockFW-Token header, compared in constant time. The token arrives in the URL fragment, which browsers never send to a server; the page moves it into session storage and strips it from the address bar. A custom header cannot be sent cross-origin without a CORS preflight, which this server never answers, so another site open in the same browser cannot read the API or ride the token. Host check Only 127.0.0.1:port and localhost:port are accepted as the Host header. A hostile name re-pointed at 127.0.0.1, the DNS-rebinding trick, is refused before any path is looked at. Read-only The API has no write endpoint. Changing the firewall stays with stockfw apply and the desktop app, which need root; the dashboard can be left running without that worry. Nothing from elsewhere The page is compiled into the binary and carries a strict Content-Security-Policy with a per-run nonce, so no external script, style, font or image can load. It works offline and sets no cookies. Small on purpose GET and HEAD only, a 16 KB cap on the request head, a ten-second socket timeout and at most 32 connections at once. There is nothing to overflow and nothing to keep busy. The API# Every endpoint is GET, needs the token, and returns JSON: PathReturns /api/summaryFirewall state, IPv6 state, rule counts, totals. /api/activityThe per-interval series for both graphs, for the requested range. /api/eventsRecent events. /api/connectionsEstablished connections with programs. /api/interfacesAdapters with counters and rates. /api/policyThe saved policy, as stockfw policy list --json prints it. /api/simulate?q=…A what-if verdict, as stockfw simulate --json prints it. From a script on the same machineCopy$ curl -s -H \"X-StockFW-Token: $TOKEN\" http://127.0.0.1:43977/api/summary A request without the token, or with a wrong one, gets 401 Unauthorized and no body worth reading. Coming in 1.0.7# The threat-intel state and the enforced lists, read-only like everything else. In progress for 1.0.7"},{"t":"Events","u":"docs/events.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["Where the events come from","where-the-events-come-from"],["The columns","the-columns"],["Filter, Monitor, Clear, Refresh","filter-monitor-clear-refresh"],["Repeated events and noise","repeated-events-and-noise"],["Right-click: from an event to a rule","right-click-from-an-event-to-a-rule"],["What else reads the events","what-else-reads-the-events"]],"x":"Events Every connection the firewall allowed or blocked, as it happens. StockFW does not capture packets; it reads the verdicts your operating system already records and shows them unchanged, so each row can be checked against the system’s own log. [[shot:events|The Events page: a count of 211 events, a filter box, the Monitor switch, Clear and Refresh, and a table of time, direction, interface, port, source, destination, protocol and service with blocked rows in red.|Blocked rows are red and carry the [BLOCKED] marker that every other part of the app keys on.]] Where the events come from# Windows The Security event log, events 5156 (the Windows Filtering Platform permitted a connection) and 5157 (it blocked one). StockFW runs wevtutil every five seconds, asking for at most ten new records in text form, splits and de-duplicates them, and hands them on. Each record carries the program path, both addresses, the ports, the protocol and the direction, so the Events page on Windows names the program. The audit policy that makes Windows write these events is switched on the first time the elevated app runs; see Enable monitoring. Linux The kernel log. StockFW’s own rules log what they drop and reject with the prefixes STOCKFW-DROP and STOCKFW-REJECT, and, since 1.0.7, the first packet of each newly allowed connection with STOCKFW-ALLOW, rate-limited to ten a second. Built for 1.0.7 The page reads those lines from the kernel ring buffer (/dev/kmsg) directly, which needs root and works on WSL, in containers and on minimal systems; lines without the prefix are ignored, so unrelated kernel messages never appear. The kernel log carries no program name, so the Program column is empty on Linux; the Overview’s connection table names programs instead. Known gap Only the ten-record cap on Windows is a sampling limit: under a flood of thousands of connections a second, the page shows a steady sample rather than every row, by design, so the app cannot be made to freeze. The system log remains the complete archive. The columns# Time (the event’s own time, in local HH:MM:SS), direction (in or out), the interfaces in and out, the local port, source and destination addresses, protocol, and the service name looked up from the port, with [BLOCKED] appended on a blocked row. On Windows there is also the program. Rows are inserted newest first by their time stamp, so an event that arrives late still lands in the right place. Verified ui-hitview-smoke Filter, Monitor, Clear, Refresh# Filter narrows the list as you type: a case-insensitive match against any column. The count reads “3 of 1200 events” while a filter is active, so an empty result is never mistaken for an idle firewall. Verified test_filter_match Monitor starts and stops reading the log. When it cannot start, because you are not root on Linux or the audit policy is off on Windows, the page says so and how to fix it instead of showing nothing. Clear empties the list and forgets which rows it has seen; Refresh reads again now. An empty list always explains itself: waiting for the first event, monitoring off, or nothing matches the filter. Repeated events and noise# Two preferences on the Interface › Events page shape the list. Hide repeated events drops a row when the same source, port and verdict arrived a moment ago, so a port scan becomes a few rows rather than thousands. Skip events that are not for the firewall filters records the firewall subsystem did not produce. Beyond that, the list keeps the newest 2,000 rows and drops the oldest; it is a window, not an archive. De-duplication happens three times for three reasons: the reader refuses to deliver the same record twice, the list refuses to insert the same row twice (so a monitor that is stopped and restarted does not repeat itself), and the preference above hides visually identical connections. Verified ui-hitview-smoke Right-click: from an event to a rule# The menu offers only what that row can express, so it can never promise a rule the policy cannot hold: Outbound rows: Block destination (all traffic), Allow destination (all traffic), and Block this service to the destination, where the service is taken from the row’s port. Inbound rows: Trust source (inbound allow), or Remove trust for this source when a trust rule for it already exists. There is no inbound-deny rule kind, because inbound is denied by default; removing the trust is what blocks the source again. On an inbound row the only offer is to trust the source. Blocking it is already the default. Choosing an entry opens a dialog that prints the exact rule, as it will be written to the policy file. Where the rule kind has an opposite-direction twin, the dialog also carries the Direction setting: one direction by default, or Bi-directional, which stages the twin as part of the same change. After Yes, the rule is saved (and taken back out if the save fails), the Policy page reloads, and the change is pushed through the apply gate. The result dialog distinguishes live from staged, not live yet: administrator privileges are required. Verified test_eventrule_builders Allowing a destination also lifts any block Sentinel staged for it, before the allow is pushed, so the two reach the firewall together; on Windows a block rule outranks an allow, so without this your Allow would have changed nothing on the wire. Verified fs-copilot-smoke What else reads the events# The Activity page counts exactly the rows this page lists, after its filters and duplicate check, each at its own time. The Copilot feeds every row into its per-program baselines and Sentinel’s detectors. The Overview’s counters and recent-blocks card are the same stream, summarised."},{"t":"Questions and answers","u":"docs/faq.html","s":"Reference","h":[["About the product","about-the-product"],["Reboot and persistence","reboot"],["Linux","linux"],["Windows","windows"],["Versions","versions"],["Something else?","something-else"]],"x":"Questions and answers About the product# Does StockFW replace Windows Firewall or iptables? No. It configures them. The engine your system already has keeps doing the filtering, which is why StockFW has no driver, needs no reboot, and leaves the firewall exactly as it was when it is closed, crashes or is uninstalled. Does anything leave my computer? Nothing. There is no telemetry, no update check, no account and no network client. The dashboard listens on 127.0.0.1 only. In 1.0.7 the threat-intel feature downloads lists, each only after you turn it on and accept its publisher’s terms. The security model spells this out. Is the Copilot an AI? Not in the sense of a language model. It is a deterministic parser for what-if questions and a set of per-program counters with explained thresholds. It works offline and cannot be prompt-injected because there is no prompt. An older About dialog says “AI-powered”; that wording is being corrected. Why is there no “deny inbound” rule kind? Because inbound is denied by default. Every inbound rule is an exception that allows something; to block a source again you remove the allow. Outbound is the mirror image: allowed by default, with deny rules as the exceptions, and an optional restrictive mode that flips it. Where is the Windows command line? Not built yet. stockfw ships on Linux in 1.0.6; the core it links against is portable, and a Windows build is intended. Planned Which licence? The repository carries two texts: the inherited GPLv2 in COPYING for the original code, and Apache-2.0 in LICENSE. The README says which applies to what, and the question of a single licence for the whole is open. Known gap Reboot and persistence# What happens to my rules at reboot? Windows Windows Firewall rules persist; StockFW’s rules are ordinary rules in it, so they are in force from boot, before any program runs. Linux iptables rules live in the kernel and are gone at boot. StockFW does not install a boot service, so the policy is in force again when you press Start firewall, run sudo stockfw start, or turn on Start firewall on program startup and start the app. To have it at boot without StockFW running, add stockfw start to a systemd unit or rc.local; a shipped unit is on the roadmap. Planned If StockFW crashes, am I unprotected? No. The engine keeps the last applied rules. What you lose is monitoring, learning and Sentinel until you start it again. Linux# iptables or nftables? StockFW drives iptables and ip6tables, which on every current distribution are the nftables-backed versions, so its rules land in nftables and nft list ruleset shows them. The 1.0.7 threat-intel sets use nft directly. A native nftables backend for the policy is planned for a later release. Planned Does it fight with ufw, firewalld, Docker or libvirt? StockFW owns its own chains (INBOUND, OUTBOUND, STOCKFW-DNAT, STOCKFW-SNAT) and replaces only those; rules other tools keep in the same tables are not touched, and the loads use --noflush. Built for 1.0.7 Two tools both setting a default policy on INPUT will still disagree about what the default is, so run one host firewall manager at a time; ufw and firewalld are alternatives to StockFW, Docker and libvirt coexist with it. Why root, and not polkit or a setuid helper? Because a desktop program that can silently rewrite the firewall is a worse posture than one that asks for root openly. StockFW never raises its own rights. More. Which distributions? Tested on Debian, Kali, Ubuntu and WSL2. Anything with GTK 3 and iptables should work; the installer checks libraries by soname, not by package name, so renamed packages do not fail it. Windows# Why two shortcuts? The ordinary one runs with your rights and can read, edit and simulate; the elevated one asks for administrator rights once and can change the firewall. Keeping them separate means a program running as you cannot borrow the elevated window. Install on Windows. Why is the installer not signed? A code-signing certificate is a cost the project has not taken on yet. Check the SHA-256 on the download page instead; it tells you the same thing a signature would. Known gap Does it work with a third-party antivirus firewall? StockFW manages the Windows engine. If another product has replaced Windows Firewall as the active firewall, StockFW’s rules exist but that product decides. Versions# Which version can I download? 1.0.5. 1.0.6 was merged into main without a package, and 1.0.7 is being developed on main on top of it. The download page explains how to build it. The app says 1.0.6 but I built the 1.0.7 branch The version string is bumped at release time, so a development build reports the last version until then. Why do these pages mark some things “Built” or “In progress”? So you can tell what is in the release you can download from what is on the way. The welcome page explains each stamp. Something else?# If the troubleshooting page does not have it, open an issue. Questions are welcome there too; they become documentation."},{"t":"Files and formats","u":"docs/files.html","s":"Reference","h":[["Where","where"],["The policy","policy-files"],["The applied journal","applied-csv"],["Preferences","stockfw-conf"],["The audit log","audit-log"],["The Trust baseline","trust-csv"],["The behaviour baseline","behavior-csv"],["Sentinel’s staging record","autoblocks"],["In the engine","in-the-engine"],["Backing up","backing-up"]],"x":"Files and formats StockFW keeps everything as plain text you can read, copy, diff and back up. There is no database and no registry blob. This page is the format reference; nothing here is needed to use the app. Where# PlatformPolicy and journalPreferences, logs, baselines Windows%LOCALAPPDATA%\\stockfw\\%APPDATA%\\stockfw\\ Linux, as your user~/.stockfw/~/.config/stockfw/ Linux, as root/etc/stockfw//root/.config/stockfw/ STOCKFW_RULES_DIR overrides the first column; XDG_CONFIG_HOME the second on Linux; and STOCKFW_AUDIT_FILE, STOCKFW_TRUST_FILE and STOCKFW_AUTOBLOCK_FILE point single files elsewhere. The policy# One file per rule kind, no extension, under inbound/ and outbound/. A file that does not exist is an empty kind. FileColumns inbound/allow-fromhost, comment inbound/allow-serviceservice, port, for, comment inbound/forwardservice, firewall port, to, port, comment outbound/allow-to, outbound/allow-from, outbound/deny-to, outbound/deny-fromhost, comment outbound/allow-service, outbound/deny-serviceservice, port, for, comment inbound/allow-serviceCopySSH, 22, 192.168.1.0/24, admin LAN HTTPS, 443, Anyone, web server inbound/forwardCopyWeb, 8080, 192.168.1.30, 80, lab box Comma-separated, one rule per line, a space after each comma on write and optional on read. The comment is the last field and may be empty. A field may not contain a comma or a line break, and the app refuses to save one that does, because it would become another field. Ports are digits only (a range is 1024:2048); a host is an address, a CIDR network, Anyone, or a host name that does not start with a hyphen. A line that does not parse is skipped at load and counted (“2 malformed policy rules were skipped”); saving writes back only the rules that parsed, and never rewrites a bad line into something else. Verified test_policy_load_validation_f1 Round trip is exact: what you save is what loads. You may edit these files by hand with the app closed; the Policy page shows the result, and nothing reaches the firewall until Apply. The applied journal# applied.csv next to the policy: the last policy the engine accepted. Apply measures its impact against this file, not against the saved policy, and rolls back to it when the engine refuses. applied.csvCopy# StockFW applied policy journal v1 inbound-allow-service, \"SSH, 22, 192.168.1.0/24, admin LAN\" outbound-deny-to, \"198.51.100.66, known bad host\" One row per applied rule: the kind, then the rule’s own line as one quoted field. A row that cannot be trusted fails the whole load, and an apply then refuses rather than roll back to something unverifiable; the dialog says so. The file is rewritten after every successful apply. If it is missing, the next apply counts every rule as added, which is also what happens on a fresh install. Verified test_apply_gate Preferences# stockfw.conf, an ini file with two groups. Keys keep the names the original program used, so a setting is easy to find: stockfw.confCopy[client] first_run=false enable_tray_icon=true minimize_to_tray=true filter/redundant=false filter/not_for_firewall=true policy_auto_apply=true policy_audit_only=false start_firewall_on_gui=false [firewall] ext_if=eth0 int_if= nat=false icmp/enable=true icmp/echo_request=true deny_packets=true block_external_broadcast=false block_non_routables=true restrictive_outbound=false log_allowed=true A missing key takes its default. The Preferences page describes each setting. The audit log# audit.log, append-only, one line per thing StockFW did to the firewall or the baselines: audit.logCopy2026-11-02T16:40:12Z | policy applied | source=Policy page mode=live +1 added (1 allow, 0 block), -0 removed (0 allow, 0 block), =8 unchanged 2026-11-02T16:41:03Z | policy held, firewall untouched | source=cli mode=audit-only +0 added ... =9 unchanged 2026-11-02T16:44:50Z | event rule added | outbound-deny-to 203.0.113.9 (Events page) live 2026-11-02T16:51:19Z | sentinel auto-block | outbound-deny-to 198.51.100.77 beacon 6 contacts, mean 30 s 2026-11-02T17:01:19Z | sentinel rule removed | outbound-deny-to 198.51.100.77: expired after 10 minutes 2026-11-02T17:05:02Z | trust-attest | /usr/bin/nc.openbsd sha256=… The three fields are the time in UTC, the action, and the detail, separated by | . Actions include policy applied, policy applied, with notes, policy apply failed, policy apply refused, policy apply: no change, policy held, firewall untouched, event rule added, event rule added (bi-directional twin), event trust removed, the sentinel … family and trust-attest / trust-forget. Line breaks and the separator inside a detail are escaped, so a tool’s error text can never read as a second, forged entry. The app only ever appends; rotation is yours. Verified test_apply_gate The Trust baseline# trust.csv: quoted CSV, eight fields per row, one row per attested path. trust.csvCopy\"C:\\Program Files\\Mozilla Firefox\\firefox.exe\",\"9f3b…c1\",\"68425616\",\"1761840000\",\"valid\",\"Mozilla Corporation\",\"1762085102\",\"1762091840\" Path, SHA-256, size, modification time (unix seconds), signature state (valid, invalid, unsigned, na, unknown), publisher, first seen, last seen. A row with a different number of fields is skipped, never the file. Verified test_trust_engine The behaviour baseline# behavior.csv: what the Copilot has learned, one fact per row, keyed by program. behavior.csvCopyfirefox,meta,1342,98304,2211840,1762080000,1762091830 firefox,rate,1762091400,7,6250,41,0 firefox,port,443,1288 firefox,port,80,41 firefox,dest,104.16.4.34,212 firefox,hour,14,301 curl,partial,1 Row kinds: meta (totals and first/last seen), rate (the connection-frequency state), port, dest and hour (counts), and partial (the profile was capped and can no longer claim novelty). Rows of an unknown kind are ignored, so an older version can read a newer file. Delete the file to start learning from scratch. Sentinel’s staging record# autoblocks: what Sentinel has staged and when, rewritten on every stage and every retirement. autoblocksCopyS,1762094279,7,198.51.100.77, Sentinel: beacon 6 contacts Four fields: S, the staging time in unix seconds, the rule kind’s number, and the rule’s own CSV line last, so its commas survive the split. At start-up, rows older than ten minutes are taken out of the policy with an audit line; fresh rows are re-armed with their original time. Deleting this file loses the tracking of staged rows, not the rows themselves, which the destination gates and the next Apply then govern. Verified fs-copilot-smoke In the engine# Windows Rules named StockFW-… are policy rules; StockFW … (with a space) are Preferences rules. StockFW owns everything with those prefixes and nothing else. wf.msc or netsh advfirewall firewall show rule name=all lists them. Linux Chains INBOUND, OUTBOUND, STOCKFW-DNAT and STOCKFW-SNAT, hooked from INPUT, OUTPUT, FORWARD (router mode), PREROUTING and POSTROUTING; log prefixes STOCKFW-DROP, STOCKFW-REJECT and STOCKFW-ALLOW. In 1.0.7, the nftables table inet stockfw_intel for threat intel, with the log prefix STOCKFW-TI. nft list ruleset shows all of it. Backing up# Copy the two directories. Restoring the policy directory onto another machine and pressing Apply reproduces the firewall there; the journal will count every rule as added, which is correct. The baselines and the audit log are per machine and worth keeping for their history, not for restoring."},{"t":"Your first ten minutes","u":"docs/first-steps.html","s":"Get started","h":[["Where to go next","where-to-go-next"]],"x":"Your first ten minutes This walk-through uses the real app on a small network, so what you see here is what you will see on your own computer. Start the app with the rights to change the firewall: StockFW (Elevated) on Windows, sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW on Linux. Read the Overview The green or red card at the top answers the question you opened the app for. On a fresh install it says the firewall is stopped; press Start firewall. The card turns green, and the two small tiles say whether IPv4 and IPv6 traffic are filtered. On Linux, if ip6tables is missing, the IPv6 tile tells you instead of pretending. After Start: protected, both families filtered, nine rules in force. Watch the Events page for a minute Every connection the firewall decided appears as it happens. Red rows were blocked. Type part of an address, a port or a program name into the filter and the list narrows while you type; the count reads “3 of 1200 events” so an empty filter result cannot be mistaken for a quiet firewall. If the list stays empty, it tells you why: monitoring is off, or nothing matched. Turn an event into a rule Right-click a row. For an outbound connection you can block or allow the destination, or block just that service to it; for an inbound one you can trust the source. A dialog prints the exact rule it is about to write, you answer Yes or No, and the result says plainly whether the rule is live or only saved for the next Apply. The right-click menu offers only what that row can express. Add a rule by hand Open Policy. Rules are grouped by kind. In Allow inbound service, press Add, choose SSH on port 22 and type your LAN, for example 192.168.1.0/24, as who it is for. The row appears with its Covers column reading “IPv4 only”, because that network is an IPv4 network. A rule for “Anyone” covers both families. Each rule says what it reaches, and why on hover. Apply, and read the impact first Press Apply policy. Before anything happens, a dialog counts what will change against what is actually live: “+1 added (1 allow, 0 block), −0 removed, =8 unchanged”. Say Yes and the rule reaches the firewall; the result dialog reports what the engine said. If the engine refuses, the last good policy is put back and the dialog says whether that worked. Every attempt is one line in audit.log. Not ready to change anything? Press Audit-only first. Apply then stages the change and writes the audit line while the firewall stays exactly as it is. Ask the simulator Open Copilot and type a question into the What-if box the way you would say it: inbound rdp from 203.0.113.50. The verdict plays in a small window, the packet travelling from the source to the wall, and the text underneath names the rule that decided it, or says that no rule matched and the default applied. Simulating writes nothing. If the verdict is BLOCKED and you want it allowed, Create This Rule prints the rule and asks. A question, not a change: nothing is written by simulating. Set up the tray Open Preferences from the bottom of the sidebar. On the Interface page, Show a tray icon and Minimize to the tray keep StockFW running when you close the window. The tray icon’s tooltip carries the firewall state, so a glance answers the question the app exists for. Where to go next# Policy explains every rule kind, what “Covers” means, and what Apply does step by step. Copilot explains insights, Sentinel and auto-block, and why none of it can open a port without you. On Linux, the command line does everything above from a terminal or a script, and the dashboard shows it in a browser."},{"t":"Install on Linux","u":"docs/install-linux.html","s":"Get started","h":[["Before you start","before-you-start"],["Install","install"],["What gets installed","what-gets-installed"],["Run it","run-it"],["Why the full path after sudo","why-the-full-path-after-sudo"],["Why there is no polkit policy or setuid helper","why-there-is-no-polkit-policy-or-setuid-helper"],["Two homes: yours and root’s","two-homes-yours-and-root-s"],["Where StockFW keeps its files","where-stockfw-keeps-its-files"],["Events on Linux","events-on-linux"],["Update","update"],["Uninstall","uninstall"],["Build it yourself","build-it-yourself"]],"x":"Install on Linux The Linux build is a small tarball: the desktop app, the stockfw command line, a menu entry and an install script. GTK comes from your distribution, so the download is under 200 KB. Before you start# An x86-64 distribution with GTK 3. Debian, Kali, Ubuntu and WSL2 are what it is tested on. iptables, and for IPv6 filtering in 1.0.7, ip6tables. On current distributions both are the nftables-backed versions, which is fine: StockFW’s rules land in nftables through them. Root, for reading the kernel log and for changing rules. Everything else works as your own user. Install# TerminalCopy$ sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS.txt --ignore-missing StockFW-1.0.5-linux-x86_64.tar.gz: OK $ tar xzf StockFW-1.0.5-linux-x86_64.tar.gz $ cd StockFW-1.0.5-linux-x86_64 $ sudo ./install.sh The script checks that every library the binary links against is present, by soname rather than by package name, because Debian and Ubuntu renamed libgtk-3-0 to libgtk-3-0t64 and a package check would wrongly fail a working system. If something is missing, it names the library and stops: TerminalCopy$ sudo apt install libgtk-3-0 libgdk-pixbuf-2.0-0 # Debian, Kali, Ubuntu $ sudo dnf install gtk3 # Fedora $ sudo pacman -S gtk3 # Arch iptables is checked separately and only warned about: without it the app still runs, reads, simulates and shows the policy; only applying needs it. What gets installed# PathWhat /usr/local/bin/StockFWThe desktop application. /usr/local/bin/stockfwThe command line and local dashboard. The two names differ only in case; they are staged apart in the tarball so a case-insensitive filesystem cannot merge them. /usr/local/share/applications/stockfw.desktopThe menu entry (Settings › StockFW). It starts the unprivileged app. /usr/local/share/icons/hicolor/{48x48,128x128,256x256}/apps/stockfw.pngIcons. /usr/local/share/doc/stockfw/LICENSE.txtLicence text. /usr/local/bin/stockfw-uninstallRemoves all of the above. Run it# TerminalCopy$ StockFW # as yourself: policy, simulator, Trust; no events, no apply $ sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW # as root: the full tool As your own user, the app can show and edit a policy, run the simulator and inspect programs, but the kernel log is unreadable without root, so the Events page says that monitoring is unavailable rather than showing an empty list, and Apply refuses with a message rather than half-applying. Why the full path after sudo# sudo does not search your PATH. It uses Defaults secure_path from /etc/sudoers, which on Debian and Kali is /usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin and does not include /usr/local/bin. So sudo StockFW reports “command not found” while plain StockFW works. The installer detects this and warns in its summary. It deliberately does not edit sudoers. Adding /usr/local/bin to secure_path would widen the search path for every command anyone runs under sudo on that machine, which is a worse trade than typing a path. Why there is no polkit policy or setuid helper# StockFW expects to already be root when it changes the firewall and never re-executes itself through sudo. A desktop program that can silently rewrite the whole firewall is a worse security posture than one that asks for root openly, so the installer ships neither a polkit rule nor a setuid wrapper. Grant privilege by launching with sudo, or through your desktop environment’s own mechanism. Two homes: yours and root’s# The policy lives in a directory that depends on who is running the program: as your user: ~/.stockfw/, with preferences and logs in ~/.config/stockfw/; as root: /etc/stockfw/, with preferences and logs in /root/.config/stockfw/. A rule you add as yourself is therefore not the rule sudo stockfw apply applies. Pick one identity for policy work and stay with it: the simplest is to run both the app and every stockfw policy command through sudo, so everything lives in /etc/stockfw. For scripts and tests, STOCKFW_RULES_DIR=/some/dir points both programs at one directory regardless of who runs them. Where StockFW keeps its files# File (as root)Holds /etc/stockfw/inbound/…, outbound/…The policy, one file per rule kind. See Files and formats. /etc/stockfw/applied.csvThe last policy the engine accepted: what Apply measures against and rolls back to. /root/.config/stockfw/stockfw.confPreferences. /root/.config/stockfw/audit.logOne line per apply, attest, forget and Sentinel action. Append-only. /root/.config/stockfw/trust.csv, behavior.csv, autoblocksThe Trust baseline, the Copilot’s learned behaviour, Sentinel’s staged blocks. In the kernel, StockFW owns the chains INBOUND, OUTBOUND, STOCKFW-DNAT and STOCKFW-SNAT, hooked from INPUT, OUTPUT, PREROUTING and POSTROUTING. On each apply only those chains are replaced; rules that Docker, libvirt or ufw keep in the same tables are not touched. Built for 1.0.7 Events on Linux# The policy logs what it drops, rejects and, since 1.0.7, newly allows, and the Events page reads those lines from the kernel ring buffer (/dev/kmsg) directly, so it works on WSL, in containers and on systems without rsyslog or journald. Built for 1.0.7 The 1.0.5 and 1.0.6 releases read /var/log/kern.log or journalctl -k instead. In both cases, root is required. Update# Unpack the new tarball and run its install.sh again. Your policy and preferences are outside /usr/local and are kept. Uninstall# TerminalCopy$ sudo /usr/local/bin/stockfw-uninstall Removes the binaries, the menu entry, the icons and the licence. It leaves your policy in /etc/stockfw or ~/.stockfw, and any rules currently loaded in the kernel: press Stop firewall or run sudo stockfw stop first if you want those gone. Build it yourself# The newest code builds with CMake and Ninja in a few commands. See Build on Linux."},{"t":"Install on Windows","u":"docs/install-windows.html","s":"Get started","h":[["Before you start","before-you-start"],["Run the installer","run-the-installer"],["What gets installed","what-gets-installed"],["Two ways to run it","two-ways-to-run-it"],["Normal: the “StockFW” shortcut","normal-the-stockfw-shortcut"],["Elevated: the “StockFW (Elevated)” shortcut","elevated-the-stockfw-elevated-shortcut"],["Enable monitoring","enable-monitoring"],["Where StockFW keeps its files","where-stockfw-keeps-its-files"],["Update","update"],["Uninstall","uninstall"],["Build it yourself","build-it-yourself"]],"x":"Install on Windows StockFW ships as a single installer that includes everything it needs. Installing takes a minute; the only decision is where to put it. Before you start# Windows 10 or Windows 11, 64-bit. Windows Defender Firewall must exist. It always does, even when another security product is installed; StockFW manages the Windows engine, not a third-party one. An administrator account for installing, and for changing rules later. Reading events and editing the policy on disk do not need it. Run the installer# Download and check itGet StockFW-1.0.5-Setup.exe and SHA256SUMS.txt from the download page. In PowerShell, Get-FileHash .\\StockFW-1.0.5-Setup.exe must print the hash listed for it. Get past SmartScreenThe installer is not code-signed yet, so Windows shows “Windows protected your PC”. Choose More info, then Run anyway. You checked the hash; that is what the signature would have told you. Accept the licence and choose a folderThe default is C:\\Program Files\\StockFW. Keep it under Program Files if you can: that folder is writable only by administrators, which is part of what makes the elevated launcher safe to use. Choose componentsMain Application is required. Desktop Shortcut adds a shortcut to the ordinary, non-elevated app. FinishThe installer can start StockFW for you. The first start is a good moment to open the elevated copy once, so monitoring gets switched on (below). What gets installed# WhereWhat C:\\Program Files\\StockFW\\StockFW.exeThe application. Its manifest says asInvoker: it runs with the rights of whoever starts it and never raises them itself. …\\StockFW\\*.dll, lib\\, share\\, etc\\The GTK 3 runtime, complete, so the app does not depend on anything else on the machine. …\\StockFW\\StockFW-elevated.vbs, .cmd, .ps1The elevated launchers. Each finds StockFW.exe next to itself and asks Windows for administrator rights with a normal UAC prompt. …\\StockFW\\README.md, CHANGELOG.mdThe project README and release notes. Start menu › StockFWStockFW, StockFW (Elevated), Documentation, Uninstall. Registry, HKLM\\Software\\StockFWThe install folder, and the usual entry under Apps & features. Two ways to run it# Normal: the “StockFW” shortcut# Runs with your own rights. You can watch events (once monitoring is enabled), browse and edit the policy, run the simulator, inspect and attest programs, and change preferences. You cannot change the firewall: pressing Apply, Start or a profile switch fails with a clear message, “Administrator privileges required”, and the change stays saved for later. Nothing is half-applied. Elevated: the “StockFW (Elevated)” shortcut# Shows a UAC prompt, then runs with administrator rights. Apply, Start and Stop, the profile switches and Sentinel’s auto-block all work. Windows itself protects this window: a program running with ordinary rights cannot send it clicks or keystrokes. How the launcher stays safe The launcher resolves every path relative to its own folder, never from %TEMP%, your profile or the registry, and it refuses to start if the GTK runtime is not beside the executable instead of searching for one elsewhere. An earlier version added a build folder to PATH; that was removed, because a user-writable folder on an administrator’s search path is how a planted DLL gets loaded. Verified test-installer-spawn.sh Enable monitoring# Windows writes a firewall event for every connection only when the audit policy asks it to. On its first elevated run StockFW enables the Filtering Platform Connection subcategory with auditpol; running it again is harmless. After that, events 5156 (allowed) and 5157 (blocked) appear in the Security log and on the Events page. If the Events page says monitoring is not enabled, open the elevated app once, or run this in an administrator PowerShell: PowerShellCopyPS> auditpol /set /subcategory:\"Filtering Platform Connection\" /success:enable /failure:enable This is the one machine setting StockFW changes that is not a firewall rule, and the app says so when it does it. Where StockFW keeps its files# Everything is yours to read, copy or delete. Nothing is hidden in a database. FileHolds %LOCALAPPDATA%\\stockfw\\inbound\\…, outbound\\…The policy, one file per rule kind. See Files and formats. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\stockfw\\applied.csvThe last policy the firewall accepted: the journal Apply measures against and rolls back to. %APPDATA%\\stockfw\\stockfw.confPreferences. %APPDATA%\\stockfw\\audit.logOne line per apply, attest, forget and Sentinel action. Append-only. %APPDATA%\\stockfw\\trust.csv, behavior.csv, autoblocksThe Trust baseline, the Copilot’s learned behaviour, and Sentinel’s staged blocks. Rules StockFW creates in Windows Firewall are named StockFW-… (policy rules) and StockFW … (Preferences rules), so you can find them in wf.msc. Update# Run the new installer over the old one. Your policy, preferences and baselines are in your profile, not in the install folder, so they are kept. Uninstall# Settings › Apps, or Uninstall in the Start menu folder. The uninstaller removes the program, the runtime and the shortcuts. It does not remove the firewall rules StockFW created or your files in %APPDATA% and %LOCALAPPDATA%; press Stop firewall in the app before uninstalling if you want the rules gone, or delete the StockFW-… rules in wf.msc. Build it yourself# The newest code builds on Windows with MSYS2 in about five commands. See Build on Windows."},{"t":"IPv6","u":"docs/ipv6.html","s":"Protection","h":[["Windows","windows"],["Linux before 1.0.7","linux-before-1-0-7"],["Linux since 1.0.7","linux-since-1-0-7"],["Which family a rule reaches","which-family-a-rule-reaches"],["What IPv6 always needs","what-ipv6-always-needs"],["Router mode","router-mode"],["What the app tells you","what-the-app-tells-you"],["Testing it without IPv6","testing-it-without-ipv6"]],"x":"IPv6 A rule that only exists for IPv4 is a door left open on every network that also speaks IPv6, which today is most of them. StockFW tells you, per rule and on the Overview, exactly which families are filtered, and on Linux since 1.0.7 it filters both from the same policy. Windows# Windows Windows Firewall rules apply to both families unless an address scopes them, so a rule for Anyone covers IPv4 and IPv6, and a rule for 2001:db8::5 covers IPv6 only. The Overview’s two tiles both read “Filtered” whenever the firewall is on. One limit: the Windows engine takes addresses, not names, so a rule whose target is a host name covers nothing there, and the Covers column says so. Known gap Linux before 1.0.7# Linux The 1.0.5 and 1.0.6 releases drive iptables only. Their IPv4 policy is complete, and IPv6 traffic is not filtered by StockFW at all: whatever the kernel’s default is, that is what happens. Both releases say so in the README and the architecture document; neither pretends otherwise in the app. Known gap If you run one of them on a network with IPv6, you are relying on your router or on another firewall for that family. Linux since 1.0.7# Linux Built for 1.0.7 The whole policy is rendered before the kernel is touched into two payloads, one for iptables-restore and one for ip6tables-restore, each with a filter part and, in router mode, a nat part. The backend snapshots StockFW’s chains in both families, loads IPv4, then IPv6, and on any failure puts back every table it changed. Each payload is loaded with --noflush and declares only StockFW’s own chains, so rules that Docker, libvirt or ufw keep in the same tables are never seen, and the hook from INPUT into INBOUND is not removed and re-added during a re-apply. Verified test-apply-dual.sh The rollback covers both families, always. The first version rolled IPv6 back and skipped IPv4 whenever the IPv6 restore itself failed, which would have left IPv4 on the new policy while the dialog said nothing changed; four “teeth” runs now pin it, each removing one part of the rollback and watching a specific check go red. Which family a rule reaches# TargetRendered into An IPv4 address or networkThe IPv4 payload only. An IPv6 address or networkThe IPv6 payload only. Anyone, or emptyBoth. A host nameLooked up once per apply and written as each family’s addresses, so no name is left for the tools to resolve while loading. A name with no address in one family gets a note. An allow whose name cannot be looked up is left out and reported (it fails closed). A block whose name cannot be looked up refuses the apply while rules are loaded, so the last good addresses stay blocked; when nothing is loaded yet (Start), it is left out and the notes begin with “NOT BLOCKED:”, because refusing would leave every host unfiltered over one name. Verified test_restore_plan A port forwardIts destination’s family, written as [2001:db8::5]:80 for IPv6. Masquerading stays IPv4-only. A destination that is a name or a range is left out and noted. The same decision drives the Covers column on the Policy page, stockfw policy list and its JSON, and a test checks that “Covers” names a family exactly when that family’s payload contains the rule. Verified test-core.sh What IPv6 always needs# Neighbour discovery is ICMPv6, not ARP, so it goes through the filter, and a firewall that drops it takes the host off the network. Ahead of every preference and never rate-limited, INBOUND accepts packet-too-big, time-exceeded and parameter-problem; router advertisements from fe80::/10, neighbour solicitations and advertisements, all with hop limit 255; multicast listener discovery with hop limit 1; and DHCPv6 replies from link-local. OUTBOUND lets the host’s own solicitations, advertisements, listener reports, packet-too-big and DHCPv6 requests out, ahead of restrictive mode’s default drop. The ICMP preferences map to ICMPv6 where a counterpart exists; timestamps, address masks and source quench are IPv4-only and labelled so. Router mode# IPv6 forwarding is hooked only in router mode, because container and VM runtimes accept forwarded traffic in their own tables and would otherwise be cut by INBOUND’s default drop. An IPv6 forward needs net.ipv6.conf.all.forwarding=1, and a forwarding host ignores router advertisements, which would drop its own default route; before turning forwarding on, the backend sets accept_ra=2 on the external interface, and only if it was 1, since 0 means someone turned advertisements off on purpose. What the app tells you# The Overview has an IPv6 traffic tile that reads the kernel: “Filtered”, or “Not filtered” with the reason in the sidebar: ip6tables not installed, chains not loaded, loaded but not hooked, or the firewall stopped. “None on this system” when the kernel has no IPv6 stack. It never infers IPv6 from IPv4. stockfw status has an IPv6 line and an ipv6 object in its JSON. The dashboard’s Firewall tile reads “In force for IPv4 and IPv6” or warns “IPv4 only”. A successful apply on a host without ip6tables is a success with a note, not a failure: “IPv6 is not filtered: ip6tables is not installed”. Install the package and Apply again. Testing it without IPv6# The project’s test container has no IPv6 stack. STOCKFW_ASSUME_IPV6=1 makes the backend load the IPv6 payload anyway, so rule loading is exercised for real in a private network namespace while packet behaviour is checked as rule text. The screenshots on this site were taken that way."},{"t":"Overview","u":"docs/overview.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["The protection card","the-protection-card"],["The counters","the-counters"],["Open connections","open-connections"],["Network adapters","network-adapters"],["Recent blocks","recent-blocks"],["Lock policy","lock-policy"],["The sidebar","the-sidebar"]],"x":"Overview The first page answers one question, “is this computer protected?”, in one sentence, and then shows the four things you would check next. It is the 1.0.7 design; the 1.0.5 release has a Status page with the same facts laid out as a strip. Built for 1.0.7 Protected, both families filtered, nine rules, one adapter, live connections with their programs. The protection card# Green with a shield when the firewall is running and your policy is in force; red when it is stopped. The sentence under the heading says what that means for traffic, and the four tiles break it down: IPv4 traffic, IPv6 traffic “Filtered” or “Not filtered”, separately. On Linux the IPv6 tile reads the real state of the kernel: if ip6tables is not installed, the IPv6 chains are not loaded, or they are loaded but not hooked, it says “Not filtered” and the sidebar shows the reason, rather than inferring IPv6 from IPv4. On Windows both read “Filtered” when the firewall is on, because Windows Firewall rules cover both families. Built for 1.0.7 Policy How many rules are saved. Saved is not the same as applied; the Policy page says which rules are live. Engine What is doing the filtering: Linux (iptables) or Windows Firewall. On Windows the card also shows the profile in force (Domain, Private or Public) and the network the computer is on, read through the firewall’s own interfaces. Built for 1.0.7 Compile-tested in CI; not yet run by hand on Windows. Start firewall puts your saved policy in force: on Linux it loads the rules, on Windows it switches the firewall profiles on. Stop firewall takes StockFW’s rules out again (Linux) or switches the profiles off (Windows), and the card turns red, because that is what it means. View events jumps to the Events page. Being fixed for 1.0.7 On Linux, Start firewall and the “start on program startup” preference used to load the firewall before the saved policy had been read, briefly applying an empty policy. 1.0.7 makes Start read the policy first and go through the same gate as Apply. The counters# Four tiles count since the app opened: inbound events (with how many were blocked), outbound events, open connections (with how many programs own them), and traffic right now across your adapters. “Idle” means no bytes in the last second. These are the same numbers the Events and Activity pages show in detail. Open connections# Every established TCP connection, with the program that owns it, the remote address, the port, the service name and the direction. On Linux the program is found through /proc/<pid>/fd; on Windows through the TCP table. When the owner cannot be read the cell shows ?, never a guess. Direction is decided by what is listening: a connection on a port that something on this computer listens on was accepted, so it is Inbound and named by that port. Only connections nobody listens for fall back to guessing from port numbers, which is what older versions did for everything and got wrong whenever both ends were high ports. Verified test-core.sh Connections between two programs on this computer are hidden by default and counted at the bottom of the table (“2 local connections are hidden · Show them”), and so are the dashboard’s own sockets, which exist only because someone is looking. Network adapters# Each adapter with its type (Ethernet, Wireless, Virtual), address, totals and current rate. Virtual adapters, which is what Docker bridges, VM networks and VPN tunnels create, are hidden by default and counted, with Show them to list them. On Linux the type comes from sysfs: a wireless extension means Wireless, a backing bus device means Ethernet, anything else is Virtual. Recent blocks# The last few blocked connections, each with the remote address, what blocked it (your policy, or Sentinel) and the time. All events opens the Events page. When nothing has been blocked since the app opened, the card says so instead of showing an empty list. Lock policy# The button in the top corner. While the policy is locked, every Apply, from any page or from the command line, becomes a dry run: the change is measured and saved, an audit line reads “policy held, firewall untouched”, and the engine is left exactly as it is. Use it when you are editing a policy you do not want to apply piecemeal. Audit-only mode on the Policy page does the same thing from the other side. Verified test_apply_gate The sidebar# The bottom of the sidebar repeats the verdict, Protected or Not protected, with the engine’s name, so it is visible from every page. Preferences and About StockFW sit above it."},{"t":"Policy","u":"docs/policy.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["How to think about it","how-to-think-about-it"],["The rule kinds","the-rule-kinds"],["Covers","covers"],["One direction or both","one-direction-or-both"],["Apply, step by step","apply-step-by-step"],["Audit-only and Lock","audit-only-and-lock"],["What is checked before anything is saved","what-is-checked-before-anything-is-saved"],["In the engine","in-the-engine"]],"x":"Policy Your rules, grouped by kind, each one a row you can read. Saving a rule changes a file in your profile; pressing Apply changes the firewall, after showing you what will change. Rules are grouped by kind. Point at a Covers cell and the tooltip says why a rule reaches the families it does. How to think about it# The engine’s defaults do most of the work: new inbound connections are denied unless a rule allows them, new outbound connections are allowed unless a rule denies them, and replies to connections that are already open always pass. StockFW rules are exceptions to those defaults, which is why the kinds are named the way they are: allow inbound, deny outbound. There is no inbound-deny kind because inbound is already denied; to stop trusting a host you remove its allow. The rule kinds# On the pageCommand-line nameFieldsMeaning Allow connections from hostinbound-allow-fromhostEverything from this address, network or host name may come in. Allow inbound serviceinbound-allow-serviceservice, port, forThis port may be reached from for: a host, a network such as 192.168.1.0/24, or Anyone. Forward serviceinbound-forwardservice, firewall port, to, portTraffic arriving on the firewall port is sent to another host and port. Router mode only; needs an external interface in Preferences, and the destination must be an address. Allow connections to hostoutbound-allow-tohostEverything to this host may go out, even in restrictive outbound mode. Allow outbound serviceoutbound-allow-serviceservice, port, forThis port may be reached outbound, for the given host or anyone. Deny connections to hostoutbound-deny-tohostNothing may go out to this host. Deny outbound serviceoutbound-deny-serviceservice, port, forThis port may not be reached outbound, for the given host or anyone. Allow / deny connections from (router mode)outbound-allow-from, outbound-deny-fromhostFor a LAN host behind this machine: what it may or may not send out through it. Every rule can carry a comment, which is kept in the file and shown in its own column. The “service” field is a name for your benefit; the port is what the engine sees. Common names (SSH, HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, RDP and so on) are known, so the simulator and the Events page can use them. Covers# A rule reaches the address families its target can reach, and the Covers column says which, with the reason as a tooltip. Built for 1.0.7 TargetCovers An IPv4 address or networkIPv4 only An IPv6 address or networkIPv6 only Anyone, or an empty fieldIPv4 + IPv6 A host nameIPv4 + IPv6 on Linux, where the name is looked up once at apply and written as each family’s addresses. Nothing on Windows, whose firewall takes addresses only. A forwardIts destination’s family. Nothing when the destination is a name or a range, which the generator leaves out. The same answer appears as [IPv4 only] in stockfw policy list and as covers and covers_why in its JSON, and a test checks that, for each kind of target, “Covers” names a family exactly when that family’s generated rules contain the rule. Verified test-core.sh One direction or both# Inbound and outbound are separate rule spaces, so allowing a peer both ways would normally take two rules. On the groups where a rule has an opposite-direction twin, the Add row carries a Direction control: Inbound only (or Outbound only) by default, or Bi-directional, which stages the twin as part of the same change. The pairs are exact: allow-from pairs with allow-to, and inbound allow-service pairs with outbound allow-service. Deny kinds and forwards have no twin, so the control is absent there rather than disabled. The pair is saved as one change: if the write fails, both rules come back out. Verified test_direction_pairing This is about new connections in the other direction. Replies to an open connection never need a rule; the engine is stateful. Apply, step by step# Save first. Adding, editing or removing a rule writes the policy file immediately, and if the write fails the change is taken back out of memory, so what you see is always what is on disk. Measure. Pressing Apply policy compares the saved policy with the journal of what the engine last accepted (applied.csv) and shows the counts: “+1 added (1 allow, 0 block), −0 removed, =8 unchanged”. Nothing has happened yet; you can say No. Push. On Linux the whole policy is rendered into an iptables-restore payload and an ip6tables-restore payload and loaded with --noflush, so only StockFW’s own chains are replaced, each family in one atomic step; if the IPv6 load is refused, the IPv4 load is undone. Built for 1.0.7 On Windows each rule is one netsh advfirewall command, and only the rules that changed are touched. Preferences that compile to rules (ICMP, broadcasts, reserved sources) are pushed in the same step. Record. On success the journal is updated to what is now live, so the next Apply measures against reality. Report. The dialog says what the engine said. On Linux a successful apply can carry notes (“ip6tables is not installed”, “the forward to 192.168.1.30 was left out: no external interface is set”); on Windows a rule the engine refused makes the apply partial, and the gate restores the last good policy and reports whether the restore worked. Audit. One line in audit.log: the source (Policy page, Events page, Copilot, Sentinel or cli), the mode, the counts, and any notes. Verified test_apply_gate Apply policy changes immediately, on the Interface › Policy preference page, skips the button: each edit is pushed as soon as it is saved, through the same gate and with the same audit line. Audit-only and Lock# Audit-only on this page and Lock policy on the Overview both hold the firewall still. While either is on, Apply measures and records the change, writes an audit line reading “policy held, firewall untouched”, and leaves the engine alone. Turn it off and press Apply when you are ready. Rehearsing a policy this way is the intended use. Verified test_apply_gate What is checked before anything is saved# Ports are digits only (so 80abc and 0x50 are refused), 0 to 65535, and a range’s first port is not above its last. Hosts are an IPv4 or IPv6 address, a network in CIDR form (192.168.1.0/24, 2001:db8::/32), or a host name; a host name may not start with a hyphen. A comma or line break inside a field is refused, because the files are plain comma-separated text and it would become a second field. Verified test_policy_load_validation_f1 When a policy file is loaded and a row does not parse, that row is skipped and counted (“2 malformed policy rules were skipped”), never guessed at or rewritten, and saving does not silently drop or change it. Verified test-core.sh In the engine# On Windows every rule StockFW creates is named StockFW-…, with the same canonical name used to add and to delete it, so a change never leaves a stale copy behind; a verified clear re-enumerates and retries until none remain. On Linux the rules live in chains StockFW owns (INBOUND, OUTBOUND, STOCKFW-DNAT, STOCKFW-SNAT) and nothing else in the tables is touched. In both cases wf.msc or nft list ruleset can confirm what StockFW says. The files behind the page are described in Files and formats."},{"t":"Preferences","u":"docs/preferences.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["Interface","interface"],["Interface › Events","interface-events"],["Interface › Policy","interface-policy"],["Firewall","firewall"],["Firewall › Network Settings","firewall-network-settings"],["Firewall › ICMP Filtering","firewall-icmp-filtering"],["Firewall › ToS Filtering","firewall-tos-filtering"],["Firewall › Advanced Options","firewall-advanced-options"],["How settings reach the firewall","how-settings-reach-the-firewall"],["Where they are stored","where-they-are-stored"]],"x":"Preferences Open it from the bottom of the sidebar or with Ctrl+,. The dialog has two groups, Interface for how the program behaves and Firewall for what the engine does. A control that cannot work on your platform is shown disabled with a sentence saying why, so you never flip a switch that does nothing. Being redesigned for 1.0.7 The dialog is being rebuilt in the look of the new Overview, on both platforms. Every setting below stays; the layout changes. Interface# The Interface page. The sidebar lists Interface, Events, Policy, Firewall, Network Settings, ICMP Filtering, ToS Filtering and Advanced Options. Show a tray icon On by default. Puts StockFW in the notification area with the firewall state in its tooltip. Minimize to the tray On by default. With the icon shown, closing or minimising the window keeps StockFW running in the background; the tray menu opens it again or quits. See Background and the tray. Interface › Events# The two filters that shape the Events list, and two lists that are honestly disabled. Hide repeated events Off by default. Drops a row when the same source, port and verdict arrived a moment ago, so a scan does not flood the list. Skip events that are not for the firewall On by default. Leaves out records the firewall subsystem did not produce. Ignored hosts, Ignored ports Shown disabled. The original program had these lists; this version has not wired them up yet, and says so instead of showing an empty box that would accept entries and ignore them. Known gap Interface › Policy# One switch, with its consequence spelled out. Apply policy changes immediately On by default. Each rule you add, edit or remove is pushed to the firewall as soon as it is saved, through the same gate and with the same audit line as the Apply button. Off, every change waits for Apply policy, and the Policy page shows what is staged. Firewall# Start/restart firewall on program startup Off by default. When the app starts, it loads the saved policy and starts the firewall. In 1.0.7 this reads the policy first and goes through the apply gate; earlier Linux versions briefly loaded an empty policy before the saved one. In progress for 1.0.7 Start/restart firewall on dial-out, on DHCP lease renewal Shown disabled: not implemented in this version. Windows starts its own firewall before any program runs, so there is nothing to restore at boot there; on Linux, see what happens at reboot. Firewall › Network Settings# Linux This page is offered on Linux only. Windows has no equivalent that the app can drive honestly, so it is not shown there rather than shown inert. The pickers list what is really attached. eth0 here is the staged network’s only adapter. Internet connected network device The external interface, or Any device. Needed for port forwards and for the “reserved addresses on public interfaces” block. The list is read from the system, so only real adapters appear. Local network connected device The internal interface in router mode. Traffic arriving on it is accepted. Enable Internet connection sharing Router mode: forwarding between the two interfaces with masquerading (IPv4 only). Port forwards need this. Enable DHCP for the local network Shown disabled: the built-in DHCP server of the original is not carried over. Firewall › ICMP Filtering# Each type says whether it exists in ICMPv6. The note under the list says what IPv6 always allows. Enable ICMP filtering turns the page on. With it off, ICMP is left to the engine’s defaults. With it on, only the ticked types may arrive: TypeDefaultNotes Echo request (ping)onLets others ping you. Linux rate-limits it to one a second. Echo reply (pong)onAnswers to your own pings. TracerouteonUDP port 33434, which Unix traceroute uses; not ICMP at all, kept here because the original kept it here. MS TracerouteonDestination-unreachable, which Windows tracert needs. UnreachableonDestination-unreachable in general. On IPv6, address-unreachable. RedirectionoffRouter redirects, rate-limited. Leave off unless you know your network uses them. Timestamping, Address Masking, Source QuenchingoffIPv4 only; ICMPv6 has no such messages, and the labels say so. Two things are never filtered while ICMP filtering is on, because the network stops working without them: time-exceeded and parameter-problem on IPv4, and on IPv6 the whole of neighbour discovery, router advertisements, packet-too-big, multicast listener reports and DHCPv6 replies. The page says so under the list. Built for 1.0.7 Windows Windows Firewall applies the same choices as rules without the rate limits the Linux backend adds. Verified test-core.sh Firewall › ToS Filtering# Linux Type-of-service marking is not wired into the rule generator in this version, so every control on this page is disabled and the page says so at the top. It is kept so the layout matches the original; nothing you set here has an effect. Known gap Firewall › Advanced Options# The Linux page. On Windows the rejection method is fixed and the logging switch is absent, with a note for each. Preferred packet rejection method Reject with error packet tells the sender at once that the connection is refused; Drop silently lets it time out. Linux only: Windows Firewall always drops silently and has no per-rule reject, and the page says so. Block broadcasts from external network, from internal network Drop broadcast traffic arriving on the external or the internal interface. Block traffic from reserved addresses on public interfaces Anti-spoofing: drop packets that arrive on the external interface claiming a private or reserved source address. Needs an external interface on the Network Settings page (Linux) or applies to the Public profile (Windows). Log allowed connections Linux 1.0.7. Logs the first packet of each newly allowed connection, at most ten a second, so the Events page and the Activity graph can show allowed traffic and not only blocks. Windows logs allowed connections through its own audit policy and has no switch here. Built for 1.0.7 How settings reach the firewall# Interface settings take effect when you press Accept. Firewall settings compile into rules, so they are pushed with the next Apply, through the same gate as the policy; on Windows they become rules named StockFW … (with a space, where policy rules have a hyphen) so the two sets can never collide. Verified test-core.sh Where they are stored# A plain ini file, stockfw.conf, with a [client] and a [firewall] group: %APPDATA%\\stockfw\\stockfw.conf on Windows, ~/.config/stockfw/stockfw.conf on Linux (/root/.config/stockfw/ when run as root). You can edit it with the app closed; a missing key takes its default. See Files and formats."},{"t":"Security model","u":"docs/security.html","s":"Protection","h":[["What it is, in one paragraph","what-it-is-in-one-paragraph"],["Three boundaries","three-boundaries"],["One gate","one-gate"],["Why a program running as you cannot change the firewall","why-a-program-running-as-you-cannot-change-the-firewall"],["What tampering with the files buys","what-tampering-with-the-files-buys"],["The Copilot cannot be talked into anything","the-copilot-cannot-be-talked-into-anything"],["What leaves your computer","what-leaves-your-computer"],["The local dashboard","the-local-dashboard"],["Honest limits","honest-limits"]],"x":"Security model StockFW is a privileged program by nature: when it is allowed to, it rewrites the firewall. This page explains the boundaries it keeps, so you can judge for yourself whether running it makes your computer safer. Every claim here is spelled out with its evidence in the architecture and security document, chapter 5 and the release addenda. What it is, in one paragraph# StockFW contains no packet filter, no driver and no service. It configures the engine your operating system already has, through the tools that system documents (netsh advfirewall and the firewall COM interfaces on Windows; iptables-restore, ip6tables-restore and nft on Linux), and it reads the verdicts that engine already logs. If StockFW is closed, crashes or is uninstalled, the engine keeps enforcing whatever was last applied. Nothing StockFW does can make the firewall less enforced by failing. Three boundaries# Enforcement Anything that changes firewall state must run with administrator or root rights, and StockFW contains no code that raises its own rights: no runas, no ShellExecute, no sudo, no setuid helper, no polkit rule. The Windows manifest says asInvoker. Elevation happens only when you choose the elevated shortcut and answer the UAC prompt, or type sudo. Verified test-installer-spawn.sh Input Text from the system (event logs), from disk (policy files, preferences, journals) and from you (the policy editor, the simulator) is untrusted until it has been parsed into typed values. Ports are digits only; addresses are parsed with inet_pton and never resolved by the engine; every generated Linux rule line passes a character whitelist before it reaches a restore payload; every Windows argument is one argv token quoted by the exact inverse of the child’s parser. There is no shell anywhere: not system(), not cmd /c, not sh -c. Verified test_restore_plan Display What the app shows is derived data. An event is text in a list, never markup and never a command, so a hostile program cannot make the Events page do something by choosing what to connect to. One gate# Every path that can change the firewall, the Apply button, an event’s right-click menu, a Copilot suggestion, Sentinel’s auto-block, the Preferences dialog and stockfw apply, calls one function. It measures the change against the journal of what is live, honours Audit-only and Lock, touches only the rules that changed, rolls back to the journal if the engine refuses and verifies the rollback instead of assuming it, and appends one line to the audit log. There is no second door, and the gate is tested with a scripted fake backend through every branch. Verified test_apply_gate Why a program running as you cannot change the firewall# The interesting attacker is not someone on the network, who can only generate traffic for StockFW to display. It is malware already running with your rights, trying to make the elevated StockFW install its rules. The walk-through from the architecture document: It rewrites the policy files with permissive rows. Nothing happens: there is no file watcher, and the files are read when the app loads them. The rows are visible on the Policy page. It presses Apply in the unprivileged window. The engine refuses, the app says “Administrator privileges required”, and the rows never reached a privileged process. It sends clicks to the elevated window. Windows’ UIPI drops input from a lower-integrity process. It plants a fake netsh.exe or iptables on the search path. The tools are resolved from the system directories, and the installed program sits in a folder only administrators can write. It races the temporary files the app uses to capture tool output. They are created atomically and reopened share-read; a planted file causes a sharing violation and the error path. Each step is a structural dead end, not a detection. The same reasoning holds on Linux with root in place of administrator, where additionally the installer never widens sudo’s search path or installs any helper. Verified test-installer-spawn.sh What tampering with the files buys# The policy and preferences are your files, in your profile, writable by you. That is a deliberate choice (back up by copying, audit with diff), and its consequence is stated plainly: file integrity is not a security boundary in StockFW; the Apply button is. If someone…Then… rewrites the policy while StockFW is closednothing changes until you open the app, see the rows, and press Apply with privileges. rewrites it while StockFW is openthe in-memory policy wins on the next save; a later load counts malformed rows; Apply still needs the button. deletes a filethe app reports it; the engine keeps its rules. replaces it with garbagerows that do not parse are skipped and counted, never half-applied. edits the journala row that cannot be trusted fails the journal load, and the apply refuses rather than roll back to a lie. Verified test_apply_gate The Copilot cannot be talked into anything# There is no language model and no prompt. The what-if parser recognises a dozen words; the behaviour analysis is counters. An attacker’s only input is the traffic it generates, which becomes rows and numbers, never instructions. Everything the Copilot proposes is printed as the exact rule in a Yes/No dialog, allow rules are never applied without that dialog, and the one autonomous action, Sentinel’s auto-block, can only add a deny for a public destination, expires in ten minutes, and is fenced as described on the Copilot page. Verified test-adversarial.sh What leaves your computer# Nothing. StockFW has no telemetry, no analytics, no update check and no network client. The only processes it starts are local system tools. Event data lives in memory and on screen; the policy records intent, not history; the audit log records what StockFW did, not what you connected to. In 1.0.7 the threat-intel feature will download lists, each only after you turn it on and accept its publisher’s terms, from that publisher and no mirror, and the consent dialog says what the publisher learns (your public address and StockFW/version). In progress for 1.0.7 One machine setting outside the firewall is changed, and disclosed when it happens: on Windows, enabling monitoring turns on the Filtering Platform Connection audit subcategory with auditpol. The local dashboard# stockfw dashboard is the one place StockFW listens on a socket. It binds 127.0.0.1 only and has no option to bind anything else; every API call needs a 128-bit token generated for that run; a request whose Host header is not 127.0.0.1:port or localhost:port is refused, which closes DNS rebinding; and the API is read-only, so even with the token a page cannot change the firewall. Verified test-cli.sh Honest limits# The installer is not code-signed yet; check the SHA-256 on the download page. Known gap There is no private disclosure address yet. Report security problems as a GitHub issue; if the details are sensitive, say so in the issue without them and ask for a private channel. Known gap The netsh output parser assumes English Windows. On another locale a refused rule is still refused; only the explanation may be less specific. Protection of your profile at rest (disk encryption) is the operating system’s job, not StockFW’s."},{"t":"What-if simulator","u":"docs/simulator.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["Where it lives","where-it-lives"],["How to phrase a question","how-to-phrase-a-question"],["How a verdict is reached","how-a-verdict-is-reached"],["The window","the-window"],["Create This Rule","create-this-rule"],["From a script","from-a-script"],["What it does not know","what-it-does-not-know"]],"x":"What-if simulator Type a sentence about a connection and the simulator tells you whether your saved policy would allow or block it, which rule decided, and why. It is a question, not a change: simulating writes nothing. Allowed by a rule. The rule’s name is the same one the Policy page and the engine use. Where it lives# In the box at the bottom of the Copilot page, in the dashboard, and as stockfw simulate on the command line. All three run the same code against the same policy files, and a test asserts that the window, the command line and the dashboard give the same verdict for the same sentence. Verified test-cli.sh How to phrase a question# The grammar is deliberately small, so what gets evaluated is always what you typed and nothing the simulator guessed. Words can come in any order. You writeIt means inbound, incoming, or from an addressTraffic arriving at this computer. With neither word, from means inbound. outbound, outgoing, or to an addressTraffic leaving this computer. an address or network: 203.0.113.50, 192.168.1.0/24, 2001:db8::5The remote end. port 22, 22 port, or a service name: ssh, rdp, https, dns, telnetThe local port for inbound, the remote port for outbound. A service name is looked up to its port; a number wins over a name when both appear. udp, icmp, pingThe protocol. Without one of these, TCP. allow ssh …, block dns …The word after allow names the service; the verb itself is not part of the question (the simulator answers what the policy does, not what you want). Sentences that workCopyallow ssh from 192.168.1.50 on port 22 inbound rdp from 203.0.113.50 outbound tcp to 198.51.100.66 port 443 outbound telnet to 203.0.113.7 inbound udp port 53 ping from 10.0.0.9 A sentence with no direction, no port and no address cannot be answered; the window says “Couldn’t understand this query” and shows examples instead of guessing. How a verdict is reached# The sentence is parsed into a scenario: direction, protocol, port, remote address, and the service or program named. The saved policy is compiled into the same firewall rules Apply would create, with the same names. The scenario is matched against those rules in the engine’s order: a matching block wins over a matching allow, a matching allow over the default, and the default is deny inbound, allow outbound. The answer names the deciding rule, or says that no rule matched and the default applied, and when the verdict is BLOCKED it suggests the exact rule that would allow it. The policy it reads is the saved one, so the simulator can rehearse a change before Apply: add a rule, simulate, and only then push. With Audit-only on, you can even press Apply and watch the counts without the engine changing. Verified test_apply_gate Blocked by the default. The text offers the rule that would allow it. The window# The verdict plays as a short animation: the packet leaves its source, reaches the wall, and either passes through to its destination or is turned back. Under it, the badge reads ALLOWED or BLOCKED, then the question as you typed it, the scenario as the simulator understood it (protocol, from, to, port, direction), the rule that decided, and the explanation. Replay runs the animation again. The window is a GTK drawing with no images, so it renders the same on every theme. Verified ui-simviz-smoke An outbound block. Replies to an open connection would not be asked about; this is a new connection. Create This Rule# When the verdict is BLOCKED and the simulator can express the rule that would allow it, Create This Rule appears. It prints the rule in a Yes/No dialog, exactly as it will be written to the policy file. After Yes the rule is saved and pushed through the same apply gate as the Policy page, and the result says whether it is live or staged for a later Apply. The simulator never creates a rule on its own, and never a block: blocking is a decision you make on the Policy or Events page. From a script# TerminalCopy$ stockfw simulate \"inbound rdp from 203.0.113.50\" BLOCKED Inbound TCP connection from 203.0.113.50 to local port 3389 (rdp) Packet inbound TCP port 3389 from 203.0.113.50 Rule Default deny (no matching rule) Why No rule in the loaded policy matches this connection. Default policy: deny inbound. To allow Allow inbound TCP port 3389 from 203.0.113.50. A simulation only: nothing was written. $ echo $? 5 The exit status is 0 for ALLOWED and 5 for BLOCKED, so a deployment script can refuse to continue when a port it needs would be closed. --json gives the verdict, scenario, rule, reason and suggestion as one object. See the command line reference. What it does not know# It answers from the policy, not from the engine. If someone added a rule directly in wf.msc or with iptables, the simulator does not see it. Known gap It reasons about new connections. Replies to an already open connection always pass, and the simulator does not model them. Threat-intel lists are not consulted yet. When they land, an address the policy allows but a list blocks will be answered “Blocked by threat intel: list”. In progress for 1.0.7"},{"t":"Threat-intel blocklists","u":"docs/threat-intel.html","s":"Protection","h":[["The feeds","the-feeds"],["Consent","consent"],["What a feed is checked for","what-a-feed-is-checked-for"],["What can never be blocked","what-can-never-be-blocked"],["How it reaches the kernel","how-it-reaches-the-kernel"],["What the kernel is really doing","what-the-kernel-is-really-doing"],["Where it shows up","where-it-shows-up"],["Files","files"]],"x":"Threat-intel blocklists A policy says what you allow. A blocklist says what the wider community has seen attacking, hosting botnet controllers or spraying brute-force logins, as address ranges that are refused on sight. 1.0.7 brings well-known feeds into StockFW, off until you turn each one on, with the same discipline as the rest of the app: nothing silent, nothing that cannot be undone, and the kernel’s real state shown rather than the configuration. Where this stands On main today: the address-range engine, the feed catalogue with its parsers and guards, the downloader, the discovery of this computer’s own addresses and DNS servers, the engine that builds and stores the sets, the nftables table and its transaction, and the Linux backend’s enforcement slots, each with its tests Built for 1.0.7. Being written: the Threat Intel page, stockfw intel, events that name the list, and the What-if answer In progress for 1.0.7. Windows enforcement follows once the Windows build has been checked on a real machine Planned; until then every Windows surface says blocklists are not available there yet. The design, with its review findings, is docs/1.0.7-threat-intel-design.md in the repository. The feeds# Every feed is off until you switch it on and accept its terms. A feed is fetched from its own publisher or not at all: no mirrors, no aggregator of StockFW’s own. Each entry carries the host it contacts, its information and terms pages, the licence where it has been verified (otherwise “See the provider’s terms”), whether it is for non-commercial use only, its format, its default direction and how often it updates. FeedWhat it listsDefault directionUpdates Spamhaus DROP, IPv4 and IPv6Networks hijacked or leased by professional spam and malware operations. Recommended.both12 h abuse.ch Feodo TrackerBotnet command-and-control servers. Recommended.both6 h DShieldThe top attacking networks seen by the Internet Storm Center. Non-commercial terms.in24 h FireHOL level 1An aggregate that already contains the three above and DShield. Turning it on next to them adds a note.in24 h blocklist.deAddresses reported by fail2ban installations.in24 h Emerging Threats compromised hostsHosts known to be compromised.in24 h CINS ArmyPoor-reputation addresses from the CINS sensor network.in24 h Tor exit relaysA policy choice, not a threat list; labelled as such.in6 h A custom feed is any URL that passes the URL rule, with a name, a format and a direction; typing the URL is your consent for that host. A local list is a CSV or plain file you import yourself, with no network involved. Direction means what it means for Windows’ stateful rules: in stops them connecting to you, out stops you connecting to them, both does both. Replies to connections you opened are never affected by an in list. Consent# The first time you turn a feed on, a dialog says which host will be contacted and how often, what that host learns (your public address and the string StockFW/version), the licence and a link to the terms, and that lists contain mistakes and how to make an exception. Only after Accept does anything download. Updates run hourly in the desktop app and from stockfw intel update for cron or a timer; a failed or refused update never removes the previous copy. What a feed is checked for# A downloaded file is text from the internet and is treated as hostile until it has passed every guard: a 64 MB size cap before it is read; strict parsing (addresses, CIDRs and a-b ranges, never names); no single IPv4 interval wider than a /8 and no IPv6 interval wider than a /16; no more than an eighth of the IPv4 space in one list; a list that is empty or mostly unparseable is refused and the set keeps what it had; and a sudden shrink is judged against the previous size. Content dates are read where a format has one, and a list older than seven update intervals is marked stale. Verified test-feed.c What can never be blocked# Before anything reaches the kernel, the engine subtracts from every set, and lists in its notes what it subtracted: loopback, link-local, multicast, the private ranges, carrier NAT, the reserved and documentation ranges, and the NAT64 prefixes; your own exceptions; this computer’s own addresses, its connected prefixes and its default gateways; the DNS servers in use, read from /etc/resolv.conf and, when those are loopback stubs, from systemd-resolved’s and NetworkManager’s real lists; the peer of your SSH session and every established inbound SSH connection, so a list cannot lock you out of the machine you are configuring it on; the feeds’ own hosts, so a list cannot block its own updates. Then, for sets that block outbound, each IPv4 interval is also added to the IPv6 set inside 64:ff9b::/96, so NAT64 does not bypass the list. Over all sets there are at most 300,000 intervals; above that the build fails and names the largest sets. How it reaches the kernel# Linux One nftables table, inet stockfw_intel, owned by StockFW and replaced in a single nft -f - transaction, so it applies whole or not at all and a failed apply leaves the previous table in force. The script goes to nft on standard input through GSubprocess; no shell ever sees an address. Three base chains at priority filter - 10 run before the iptables policy, so a listed address is refused even if a policy rule would have allowed it. Inbound drops are silent to the outside; outbound blocks reject with administratively prohibited, so a local program fails at once instead of hanging. Each blocking rule logs with the prefix STOCKFW-TI list, rate-limited, and carries a counter. Verified test-intelset.sh The policy and the lists never touch each other’s tables. Applying, reloading or clearing the policy leaves the threat-intel table alone. The user-facing Stop firewall suspends the lists as well, and Start resumes them, because “stop the firewall” should mean what it says. On Linux nothing survives a reboot, policy or lists, until Start is pressed; the page and the FAQ say so. What the kernel is really doing# The table carries a digest of the rule set that produced it. Every surface compares the live table with what StockFW last applied and shows one of these states, never just the configuration: StateMeaning offThe master switch is off, or no list is enabled. enforcedThe live table is the one StockFW last applied. not loadedSomething should be enforced but the table is missing: a reboot, or someone ran nft flush ruleset. differsA table is there, but not the one StockFW applied. suspendedYou stopped the firewall, so the lists are off on purpose. unsupported, needs root, unknownCannot enforce here, cannot read the kernel, cannot tell. Counters survive updates: before a set is replaced, its live count is added to the list’s carried total, so “blocked 1,204 since 3 November” stays true. Where it shows up# Threat Intel page: the state in words, the master switch, each feed with its licence, direction, entry count, content age, status and blocks; Update now and Apply; local lists; exceptions; a lookup box; the notes from the last build. Events: a block by a list shows the list’s name as its rule. For such an address the right-click Allow becomes Add exception, because an allow rule would not beat the list. Overview: one line with the state and how many lists are enforced. What-if: an address the policy allows but a list blocks is answered “Blocked by threat intel: list”. Command line: stockfw intel status | feeds | enable | disable | update | apply | clear | import | remove | except | unexcept | exceptions | lookup | on | off, each with --json; stockfw status gains an intel object. Audit log: every apply, update, consent, exception and suspend is a line. Files# Linux /var/lib/stockfw/intel/, owned by root, mode 0700: intel.conf (switches, feeds, custom feeds, local lists, exceptions), one .list and one .state per list, and applied.state for what the kernel was last given. Files are opened without following symlinks, read with a size cap, and w"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html","s":"Reference","h":[["Privileges","privileges"],["Events","events"],["Policy and Apply","policy-and-apply"],["IPv6","ipv6"],["Copilot and Sentinel","copilot-and-sentinel"],["Tray and window","tray-and-window"],["Command line and dashboard","command-line-and-dashboard"],["Uninstalling and starting over","uninstalling-and-starting-over"],["Reporting a problem","reporting-a-problem"]],"x":"Troubleshooting StockFW tries to say what is wrong in the place where it is wrong. This page collects those messages, says what each one means, and what fixes it. Privileges# “Administrator privileges required”, “Not live yet: run `sudo stockfw apply’”, exit status 3 You are changing the firewall without the rights to do it. The change is saved and will be applied the next time Apply runs with rights. On Windows use the StockFW (Elevated) shortcut; on Linux run sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW or sudo stockfw apply. Nothing was half-applied. “sudo: StockFW: command not found” Linux sudo does not search /usr/local/bin on Debian, Kali and Ubuntu. Type the full path: sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW. The installer warns about this and deliberately does not edit sudoers; see why. “Windows protected your PC” when running the installer The installer is not code-signed yet. Check the SHA-256 against the download page, then More info, Run anyway. Events# “Monitoring is not available” on Windows The audit policy that makes Windows write firewall events is off. Open the elevated app once; it enables it. Or run auditpol /set /subcategory:\"Filtering Platform Connection\" /success:enable /failure:enable in an administrator PowerShell. See Enable monitoring. If a domain policy forces auditing off, StockFW cannot override it and says so. “Monitoring is not available” on Linux The kernel log needs root. Start the app with sudo, or add yourself to the adm group for the command line’s events. The list stays empty although the firewall is running Read the sentence under the empty list. “Waiting for the first event” means nothing has been blocked or logged yet; try an inbound connection from another machine to a closed port. On Linux, allowed connections are logged only from 1.0.7 and only with Log allowed connections on; before that, the page shows drops and rejects only. No events in WSL2, a container or a network namespace Linux The kernel delivers firewall log lines from a non-initial network namespace only when net.netfilter.nf_log_all_netns=1. Set it on the host: sudo sysctl -w net.netfilter.nf_log_all_netns=1. WSL2’s kernel also needs the netfilter log modules; a stock Ubuntu WSL image has them. The Program column is empty Linux The kernel log carries no program name. The Overview’s connection table names programs for connections that are open; the Events page cannot for ones that are not. Known gap Policy and Apply# A rule I added is not applied Linux Check which policy you edited. As yourself the policy is ~/.stockfw; as root it is /etc/stockfw. stockfw status prints the directory in use. Pick one identity for policy work; see Two homes. “N malformed policy rules were skipped” A line in a policy file did not parse: a non-numeric port, a host name starting with a hyphen, a stray comma. The line was skipped, not guessed at. Open the file named in the message, fix or delete the line, reload. Saving from the app writes only the rules that parsed. The format. Apply says “policy held, firewall untouched” Audit-only on the Policy page or Lock policy on the Overview is on. The change was measured and recorded; the engine was left alone. Turn the hold off and Apply again. Apply failed and “the last good policy was restored” The engine refused a rule. The dialog carries the tool’s own message; on Windows it is often a target netsh cannot parse, on Linux a rule the kernel lacks a module for. The policy on the wire is what it was before you pressed Apply. The audit log has the line. “The forward to 192.168.1.30 was left out: no external interface is set” Port forwards need router mode: set the Internet connected network device and Enable Internet connection sharing on the Network Settings preference page. “NOT BLOCKED: name could not be looked up” A deny rule’s target is a host name that did not resolve while the firewall was being started, so it was left out rather than refuse to start at all. Fix the name or use the address, then Apply again; while the firewall is running, the same failure refuses the apply and keeps the old addresses blocked instead. A host-name rule covers nothing on Windows Windows Windows Firewall takes addresses only. Use the address, or one rule per address. The Covers column warns. IPv6# The IPv6 tile reads “Not filtered: ip6tables is not installed” Linux sudo apt install iptables brings ip6tables on Debian-family systems; dnf install iptables-nft on Fedora. Then Apply again. Until then only IPv4 is filtered, and the app says so rather than pretending. 1.0.5 and 1.0.6 never filter IPv6 on Linux. Known gap “None on this system” The kernel has no IPv6 stack. Nothing to filter, nothing wrong. Copilot and Sentinel# Sentinel blocked something I use Select the finding and press Undo auto-block, or right-click the destination on the Events page and Allow destination: your Allow lifts the block and the two reach the firewall together. The block would have expired by itself in ten minutes; it cannot come back in this session. If it happens often, set Sentinel to Detect only, which takes every staged block down at once. Sentinel never blocks loopback, your LAN, multicast or a destination the program has used before; if it did, that is a bug worth reporting with the audit lines. No insights appear The baseline needs history: four distinct hours before it judges hours, eight ten-minute windows before it judges rate. Novelty findings go quiet for a program whose profile is marked partial. Run analysis only reports what the policy and the events so far support. Tray and window# No tray icon Your desktop has no tray host (GNOME without the AppIndicator extension, or a headless session). The window works as usual and closing it quits. See Background and the tray. I closed the window and the app is still running That is Minimize to the tray. Open it from the tray icon, or quit from the tray menu. Quitting does not stop the firewall. Command line and dashboard# “cannot listen on 127.0.0.1:8080” The port is taken. Leave out --port and the system picks a free one. The dashboard did not open a browser Under sudo StockFW will not start a browser as root. Copy the printed URL into your own browser. The token is in it. The dashboard page shows “Unauthorized” The browser tab lost the token: the #token=… fragment was cut from the URL before the page loaded, the tab was reopened in a new session, or the URL is from an earlier run. Start again and open the freshly printed URL. stockfw: error: unknown command: intel You have 1.0.6. stockfw intel arrives with 1.0.7. In progress for 1.0.7 Uninstalling and starting over# Rules are still in the firewall after uninstalling By design the uninstaller leaves the engine as it is. Press Stop firewall before uninstalling, or afterwards: on Linux sudo iptables -D INPUT -j INBOUND; sudo iptables -F INBOUND; sudo iptables -X INBOUND and the same for OUTBOUND and the nat chains; on Windows delete the StockFW- rules in wf.msc. Start from scratch Stop the firewall, close the app, delete the two directories listed in Files and formats. The next start is a first run. Reporting a problem# Open an issue on GitHub with the version (About StockFW or stockfw version), the platform, what you did, what the app said, and the relevant lines of audit.log. Nothing in the audit log is secret, but read it before pasting; it names your rules’ targets."},{"t":"Trust","u":"docs/trust.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["Inspect a file","inspect-a-file"],["Scan running processes","scan-running-processes"],["Attest and forget","attest-and-forget"],["The baseline file","the-baseline-file"],["What it is for","what-it-is-for"]],"x":"Trust A firewall decides by address and port, but the question behind most of its events is “do I trust the program that made this connection?”. The Trust page answers that for one file at a time: who signed it, what its hash is, and whether it is still the file you approved. Three attested programs, all in sync with their baseline. On Linux the signature line reads “not checked” rather than guessing. Inspect a file# Type or paste a path and press Inspect. The report shows the file, its publisher, its signature state, the SHA-256 of its bytes, size, modification time, and how it compares with the baseline. Hashing a large executable takes a moment and runs off the main thread; the page says “Hashing and checking the signature…” and refuses a second task until the first finishes, so two reports can never be mixed up. Verified ui-trustview-smoke Signature lineMeaning Signed and verifiedAn Authenticode signature whose digest matches the file and whose chain stands. Signed but invalidThere is a signature, but it does not hold: the file was edited after signing, the certificate expired or is distrusted. This is the interesting negative. Not signedA Windows executable with no signature. Not an executableNo MZ header. Hashing it still makes sense; signing does not apply. Signature not checkedNo signature provider on this platform. This is what Linux shows, honestly, instead of “unsigned”. Windows The check uses WinVerifyTrust and CryptQueryObject with revocation lookups turned off, so it never touches the network and never hangs on a machine without one. The publisher is the signer’s display name. Verified test_trust_engine Linux There is no platform signature to read, so the page offers the hash, the file facts and the baseline. Package-manager signatures (dpkg, rpm) are not consulted. Known gap Scan running processes# Scan processes lists the executable behind every process this account can see and assesses each against the baseline, so a new or changed program shows up without you knowing its path. On Linux the paths come from /proc/<pid>/exe; processes owned by other users are readable only as root, and the status line says so when the list is empty for that reason. Attest and forget# Attest records the inspected file, its hash, its publisher and the time in the baseline, after a dialog that shows the path and the hash and says that the attestation goes to the audit log. From then on the file’s row reads one of: in sync: the bytes on disk match the attested hash; CHANGED: same path, different bytes. The report says which hash the baseline remembers. An update you installed produces this too, which is the point: you look, and attest again if it was you; missing: attested earlier, now gone; unreadable: the file could not be read at all; new: never attested. Forget removes a path from the baseline, with the same kind of confirmation. Refresh list reassesses every row. Both attest and forget append a line to audit.log (trust-attest, trust-forget). If the baseline cannot be written, the page says “Attested in memory, but not saved to disk” rather than pretending. The baseline file# trust.csv in your profile folder, one quoted row per path: path, SHA-256, size, modification time, signature state, publisher, first seen, last seen. Paths compare without regard to letter case, because Windows paths do. A row that does not parse is skipped, never the whole file; a missing file is an empty baseline. STOCKFW_TRUST_FILE points the app and the tests at another file. See Files and formats. Verified test_trust_engine What it is for# Trust does not change the firewall. It gives you a second kind of evidence next to the Events page: when the Copilot reports a program doing something new, Inspect tells you whether the program itself is still what it was. The two together are how you tell an update from a replacement."},{"t":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html","s":"Changelog","h":[["{{next}} · in development","v1-0-7"],["Built","v1-0-7-built"],["In progress","v1-0-7-progress"],["1.0.6 · merged into main, unreleased","v1-0-6"],["1.0.5 · 11 October 2026 · the hardening release","v1-0-5"],["1.0.4 · October 2026","v1-0-4"],["1.0.3 · the GTK3 port","v1-0-3"]],"x":"Each release also adds a chapter to the architecture and security document, which records the evidence behind every claim below. This page is the short version. 1.0.7 · in development# On branch main, unreleased. Items marked Built for 1.0.7 are committed and tested; items marked In progress for 1.0.7 are being written. Built# IPv6 is filtered on Linux. The whole policy is rendered before the kernel is touched, loaded with iptables-restore and ip6tables-restore, and if the IPv6 half is refused the IPv4 half is put back, so the kernel never runs half a policy. Neighbour discovery, router adverts, path-MTU and DHCPv6 replies are always allowed. Each address goes to its own family; a host name is looked up once and written as both. Verified test-apply-dual.sh What each rule covers. A Covers column on the Policy page (IPv4 only, IPv6 only, IPv4 + IPv6) with the reason as a tooltip; an IPv6 line on the Overview; covers and covers_why in stockfw policy list --json; the dashboard’s firewall tile says “In force for IPv4 and IPv6” or warns “IPv4 only”. Router mode fixed. Port forwards and masquerading moved into their own chains, replaced atomically on each apply; 1.0.6 appended to PREROUTING and POSTROUTING every time and never removed anything. Generator bugs fixed on the way: outbound host rules were never rules at all (missing -A OUTBOUND); hyphen port ranges were refused by iptables; router-mode denies were bypassed; forwards were dropped after DNAT. Linux events read the kernel ring buffer (/dev/kmsg) instead of depending on rsyslog or journald, so Events works on WSL, in containers and on minimal installs. Allowed new connections are logged too (STOCKFW-ALLOW, rate-limited), so the Activity graph’s allowed line is drawn on Linux. An empty list says why it is empty. The redesigned desktop app. A dark sidebar replaces the tab band; a new Overview page answers “is this computer protected?” in one sentence with IPv4 and IPv6 status, counters, open connections with their programs, adapters and recent blocks; every other page is rebuilt as cards. Virtual adapters are hidden by default and counted. Windows Overview shows the firewall profile in force and the network the computer is on, through INetFwPolicy2 and INetworkListManager. Compile-tested in CI; not yet run by hand on Windows. Threat-intel core: address-range arithmetic (fs-iprange), the feed catalogue with its parsers and guards (fs-feed), the downloader (fs-fetch), discovery of this computer’s own addresses and DNS servers (fs-hostnet), the engine that builds and stores the sets (fs-intel), the nftables table inet stockfw_intel loaded in one transaction (fs-intelset), and the Linux backend’s enforcement slots, with protected ranges that can never be blocked. Verified test-intelset.sh A new logo (flame, gear and SF shield), generated into every size the app, installer and dashboard use from one master file. Continuous integration: Linux and Windows builds and test suites on every pull request, with the Windows installer kept as a run artifact. In progress# Threat-intel feeds (Spamhaus DROP, abuse.ch Feodo Tracker, DShield, FireHOL, blocklist.de, Emerging Threats, CINS Army, Tor exits): opt-in per feed with its terms shown, strict parsing with size and coverage caps, a Threat Intel page, stockfw intel, and events that say which list blocked a connection. Country and network-owner (ASN) blocking from the IPFire location database, verified by its signature, with an anycast warning in the picker. Background mode and a richer tray menu; the Preferences dialog and the What-if window redesigned to match the new look; a smarter What-if parser. A Windows rule-translation fix: service rules for protocols other than TCP no longer open all ports. Chapter 14 of the architecture document, and the version bump. 1.0.6 · merged into main, unreleased# Activity page with two graphs over 15 minutes to 24 hours: events per minute (allowed and blocked) and adapter throughput, a crosshair, a table view, and a shaded “no data” band for time before the app started. Series are kept in 10-second slots for 24 hours at a fixed memory cost. Verified fs-activity-smoke The stockfw command line on Linux: status, policy list|add|remove, apply [--dry-run], start, stop, simulate, events [-n N] [-f] [--blocked], connections, interfaces [--watch], dashboard; --json everywhere; exit codes 0, 1, 2, 3 and 5. Verified test-cli.sh The local dashboard: a read-only page served from the binary on 127.0.0.1 only, behind a per-run token carried in the URL fragment and a custom header, with a Host check against DNS rebinding, GET and HEAD only, request-size and connection caps, and a per-run CSP nonce. Never opens a browser as root. Linux events made truthful: the right fields in the right columns, the [BLOCKED] marker every consumer keys on, the kernel’s own time stamp, lines split across reads carried over. Real network and connection panels on Linux from /proc/net/dev, sysfs and /proc/net/tcp, with the owning program found through /proc/<pid>/fd, and direction decided by the LISTEN table rather than guessed from port numbers. Desktop fixes: literal mnemonics on buttons, a Windows path suggested on Linux, frame titles striking through their borders, a blank Events list that now says whether it is waiting, off or filtered to nothing, and a window that opened too small. The Linux installer ships and installs the command line. 1.0.5 · 11 October 2026 · the hardening release# Sentinel “no false outages”. After a 2026-10-10 incident in which auto-block locked the machine out of legitimate sites, six fixes: destination gates (never your own network, multicast, loopback, link-local or a destination the program already used regularly), a session cooldown so an expired block is not re-staged a second later, persisted staging reconciled at start-up with the remaining time honoured, the user’s Allow lifting Sentinel’s block, a text-format netsh parser with verified clearing (the old one deleted nothing and stacked 479 rules), and detect mode lifting every staged block. Verified test_sentinel_auto_gates Application trust engine (the Trust page): streamed SHA-256, Authenticode verdict and signer on Windows, a baseline with five honest outcomes (new, unchanged, changed, gone, unreadable), attest and forget with audit lines, and a scan of running processes. Explainable behaviour analysis: per-program baselines of ports, destinations, hours and rate, persisted across restarts; one finding per event listing every signal with its numbers; bounded tables that go quiet rather than cry wolf when full. Safe policy enforcement: one apply gate for every path, an applied-policy journal, impact measured against what is live, a narrow delta path, audit-only testing mode, the Lock honoured at every apply site, verified rollback, and one audit line per attempt. What-if simulator developed from a stub into the Copilot’s centrepiece, with verdicts that follow Windows Firewall precedence (block beats allow, shadowed allows reported), CIDR containment, and the Simulation Visualizer window. The conversational “AI Assistant” box was removed. Bi-directional rules: a Direction setting on pairable rule kinds that stages the opposite-direction twin as one all-or-nothing save. System tray with close-to-tray behind a gate that fails closed, and the two Preferences switches made real. Verified fs-tray-smoke Windows Preferences made real on Windows: ICMP Filtering and Advanced Options compile to profile-scoped firewall rules through a portable settings plan; pages Windows cannot honour stay hidden rather than inert. The P4 adversarial suite: 49 checks over seven named scenarios (blocked port scan, single-host sweep, C2 beacon, the incident replay, benign developer tools, exfil burst, rule rollback). Verified test-adversarial.sh Linux first-run fixes: sudo’s search path explained instead of patched, a leaking iptables probe silenced, a profile box Linux could never honour hidden for good"},{"t":"Download","u":"download.html","s":"Download","h":[["Current release: StockFW {{release}}","release"],["Check the download","verify"],["Requirements","requirements"],["Newer than the release","newer"],["Build on Windows (MSYS2)","build-windows"],["Build on Linux","build-linux"],["Uninstall","uninstall"],["Older versions","older"]],"x":"Current release: StockFW 1.0.5# Released 11 October 2026, “the hardening release”. Both downloads come from the project’s GitHub Releases page; this site does not host files. PlatformFileSizeNotes Windows 10 and 11, 64-bitStockFW-1.0.5-Setup.exe44.6 MBNSIS installer. Bundles the GTK3 runtime, so nothing else needs installing. Linux x86-64StockFW-1.0.5-linux-x86_64.tar.gz194 KBTarball with install.sh. Needs GTK3 and iptables from your distribution. ChecksumsSHA256SUMS.txt192 BSHA-256 of both files. Not signed yet The Windows installer is not code-signed, so SmartScreen will show “Windows protected your PC”. Choose More info, then Run anyway, after you have checked the checksum below. Code signing is on the roadmap; until then the checksum is your verification. Check the download# Compare the output with the matching line in SHA256SUMS.txt. PowerShell (Windows)CopyPS> Get-FileHash .\\StockFW-1.0.5-Setup.exe -Algorithm SHA256 Terminal (Linux)Copy$ sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS.txt --ignore-missing StockFW-1.0.5-linux-x86_64.tar.gz: OK Requirements# WindowsWindows 10 or 11, 64-bit. Windows Defender Firewall must be present (it always is). To change rules, run the StockFW (Elevated) shortcut; everything else works without administrator rights. LinuxAn x86-64 distribution with GTK 3 (libgtk-3-0) and iptables. For IPv6 filtering in 1.0.7, ip6tables as well. Tested on Debian, Kali, Ubuntu and WSL2. Reading events and applying rules need root; sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW is the full tool. Step-by-step instructions: Install on Windows, Install on Linux. Newer than the release# Two versions sit ahead of 1.0.5 on GitHub. Neither has an installer yet; both build from source in a few commands. VersionWhereWhat it adds 1.0.6merged into main, no packageThe Activity page with its graphs, the stockfw command line and local dashboard on Linux, truthful Linux events, real network and connection panels on Linux. See the changelog. 1.0.7 (in development)branch mainIPv6 on Linux, the redesigned app, the Covers column, events from the kernel ring buffer, the new logo. Threat-intel blocklists and country blocking are being built. See what is coming. Build on Windows (MSYS2)# Install MSYS2, open the UCRT64 shell (as administrator if you want to apply rules from the build), then: MSYS2 UCRT64 shellCopy$ pacman -S --needed base-devel git mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-{toolchain,gtk3,pkg-config,ninja,cmake} $ git clone https://github.com/Cazo-Net/StockFW.git # main: the 1.0.7 development code $ cd StockFW/port $ cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja $ cmake --build build $ ./build/StockFW.exe To make an installer from the build, run installer/build_installer.sh; it stages the GTK runtime beside the executable and fails if any launcher still carries old branding. Build on Linux# Debian, Kali, Ubuntu, WSL2Copy$ sudo apt install build-essential cmake ninja-build git libgtk-3-dev iptables $ git clone https://github.com/Cazo-Net/StockFW.git # main: the 1.0.7 development code $ cd StockFW/port $ cmake -S . -B build-linux -G Ninja $ cmake --build build-linux $ sudo ./build-linux/StockFW # the desktop app $ ./build-linux/stockfw status # the command line To make the same tarball the release ships, run installer/linux/build_installer_linux.sh after building. Version strings A build from the 1.0.7 branch still reports 1.0.6 in About and --version until the release bump, which is the last commit before a release. The features are there; the number lags. Uninstall# WindowsSettings → Apps, or the Uninstall shortcut in the StockFW Start menu folder. Firewall rules StockFW created stay in Windows Firewall; remove them with Stop firewall in the app first, or in wf.msc (they are all named StockFW-…). Linuxsudo /usr/local/bin/stockfw-uninstall removes the binaries, the menu entry and the icons. Your policy in ~/.stockfw or /etc/stockfw is kept; delete it yourself if you want it gone. Older versions# Every tagged release, with its notes, is on the GitHub Releases page. The changelog describes what changed in each."}];
